We will now have the Cambridge
Rindge and Latin High School

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:15.650
choir led by Mr. Donald
Patterson, musical director,

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:20.120
lead us in singing "Lift Every
Voice and Sing" by James Weldon

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:21.530
Johnson.

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:24.620
We ask that you join in
and sing the first verse.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:27.849
Please stand and join
hands as the song is sung.

00:19:37.829 --> 00:19:52.465
[MUSIC - "LIFT EVERY VOICE AND
 SING"]

00:19:52.965 --> 00:19:56.292
(SINGING) Lift up
your voice and sing.

00:19:56.292 --> 00:20:00.284
Till earth and heaven ring.

00:20:00.284 --> 00:20:07.270
Ring with the
harmonies of liberty.

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:15.753
Let our rejoicing rise high
as the list'ning skies.

00:20:15.753 --> 00:20:23.737
Let it resound loud
as the rolling sea.

00:20:23.737 --> 00:20:30.057
Sing a song full of the
faith that the dark past

00:20:30.057 --> 00:20:31.222
has taught us.

00:20:31.222 --> 00:20:37.210
Sing a song full of the
hope that the present

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:40.204
has brought us.

00:20:40.204 --> 00:20:48.687
Facing the rising sun
of the new day begun.

00:20:48.687 --> 00:20:55.673
Let us march till
victory is won.

00:20:55.673 --> 00:21:04.156
Stony the road we trod,
bitter the chast'ning rod,

00:21:04.156 --> 00:21:12.140
felt in the day that
hope unborn had died.

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:19.625
Yet with a steady beat,
have not our weary feet,

00:21:19.625 --> 00:21:28.108
come to the place on
which our fathers sighed?

00:21:28.108 --> 00:21:36.092
We have come over a way that
with tears has been watered.

00:21:36.092 --> 00:21:42.579
We have come treading our
path through the blood

00:21:42.579 --> 00:21:45.074
of the slaughtered.

00:21:45.074 --> 00:21:51.561
Out of the gloomy
past, till now we

00:21:51.561 --> 00:22:00.044
stand at last where the white
gleam of our bright star

00:22:00.044 --> 00:22:01.042
is cast.

00:22:07.529 --> 00:22:09.525
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:36.673
GOMEZ: Thank you Mr. Donald
Patterson, musical director,

00:22:36.673 --> 00:22:38.590
and the Cambridge Rindge
and Latin High School

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:41.860
choir for that outstanding
rendition of "Lift Every Voice

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:43.470
And Sing."

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:46.520
And we have one more member of
the MLK committee, Ms. Tobie

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:47.940
Weiner.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:50.790
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:57.830
We will now begin the
program with the invocation

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:00.560
from Reverend John Wuestneck.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:03.740
Following the invocation,
breakfast will begin.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:06.500
After breakfast, we will
have two students, Georgette

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:11.360
Charles, class of 2003, and Eric
Caulfield, a graduate student,

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:15.830
guide us in reflections on the
life and legacy of Dr. King.

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:20.900
Dean Robert Redwine will present
the 2001-2002 Martin Luther

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:24.100
King Jr. Leadership Awards.

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:26.260
We will then have
another musical selection

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:29.080
from Mr. Donald Patterson and
the Cambridge Rindge and Latin

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.120
High School choir.

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:34.030
Then we will hear remarks
from Dr. Charles M. Vest,

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:36.160
and he will have the
pleasure of introducing

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:39.430
our keynote speaker,
Mr. Tavis Smiley.

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:42.580
Following the keynote speaker,
Provost Robert A. Brown

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:45.580
will recognize our Martin
Luther King Jr. Visiting

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:50.070
Professors for the 2001
and 2002 academic year.

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:52.710
Now let us begin our program
with the invocation by Reverend

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:54.150
John Wuestneck.

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:57.120
Right after Reverend
Wuestneck's invocation,

00:23:57.120 --> 00:23:58.390
we will begin breakfast.

00:24:02.272 --> 00:24:03.230
WUESTNECK: Let us pray.

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:09.890
Almighty God, bless
to us our gathering

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:13.990
on this special
occasion of celebration.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:18.890
Lift us into dignity
beyond background, color,

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:25.790
temperament of mind, and beyond
accustomed ways of seeing.

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:30.770
Remind us of the privilege we
have to gather, of the gifts

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:36.200
we have to share all
graciously given.

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:40.280
Bind us together to use
our many and varied talents

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:43.750
for the benefit of all.

00:24:43.750 --> 00:24:45.760
And bring us to the
dedication that we

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:51.100
can make a difference
in endless poverty,

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:58.520
in places of social disgrace,
in the violence of our world,

00:24:58.520 --> 00:24:59.730
and in our own lives.

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:05.390
And help us to
find creative ways

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:07.160
to benefit all human beings.

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:14.850
Bless to us, O God, the
food which feeds our bodies.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:17.100
Lift up those who
grew and raised

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:22.250
the food and the
people who prepared it.

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:27.080
Lift those also who helped with
the setting and presentation

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:31.430
of this feast and those who
will clean long after we

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:34.950
are gone from this space.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:38.550
All these prayers we lift
to you, O God, and also

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:44.590
the silence and the yet emerging
concerns from the very centers

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:46.500
of our being.

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:48.925
Amen.

00:25:48.925 --> 00:25:50.550
GOMEZ: Ladies and
gentlemen, I hope you

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.410
have enjoyed your breakfast.

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:56.220
We will now continue with
the rest of the program.

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:58.760
I have the pleasure
of introducing

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:02.180
two of our very own students
Georgette M. Charles,

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:05.900
a junior in biology, and Eric
Caulfield, a graduate student

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:09.050
in electrical engineering
and computer science.

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:11.990
They will guide us in a
reflection of the life

00:26:11.990 --> 00:26:13.820
and legacy of Dr. King.

00:26:16.395 --> 00:26:18.270
We will hear first from
Georgette M. Charles.

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:54.290
CHARLES: Distinguished
faculty, guests, students,

00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:59.360
administrators, and our gracious
hosts, President and Mrs. Vest,

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:02.690
I come before you as a
humble servant of God

00:27:02.690 --> 00:27:05.960
to reflect on the life and
legacy of Dr. Martin Luther

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:07.490
King.

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:11.090
When I was asked to give this
speech, the first question

00:27:11.090 --> 00:27:14.150
that I had in my
mind was, what do I

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:18.892
want people to open their
eyes, minds, and hearts to?

00:27:18.892 --> 00:27:21.370
In starting with
this reflection,

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:25.510
I went back to the principles
of Dr. King, who always believed

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:27.100
in knowing where
you are coming from

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:31.620
and where you are before even
mentioning where you are going,

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:34.990
so that is what I will do.

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:36.800
To tell you where
I'm coming from,

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:40.750
I will start by saying, I am
an undergraduate here at MIT,

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:43.210
a junior majoring
in biology, minoring

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:46.680
in environmental
health and toxicology.

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:49.230
Additionally, I'm the
current Vice Chairperson

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:51.630
of the MIT Chapter
of the National

00:27:51.630 --> 00:27:54.790
Society of Black Engineers.

00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:59.320
But today, I stand before
you as a representative

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:01.950
of neither standing.

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:04.210
They are mere
undercurrents of who I am

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:06.960
and who I consider myself to be.

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:11.120
Today, before you, I
represent a leader,

00:28:11.120 --> 00:28:14.040
and no one has to
verify that fact.

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:16.700
I know that I am a leader
because I know when to start

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:19.580
my day and when to end it.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:23.640
I don't think that I can
compare myself to Dr. King,

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:27.570
but I will remark,
he is a leader also--

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:31.010
not was, but is.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:32.500
In the words of
Coretta Scott King,

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:36.110
"He remains a shining example
of a discerning leader,

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:40.420
one who shows good
judgment and insight."

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:43.330
There are many leaders
around me, but how many

00:28:43.330 --> 00:28:46.150
are as astute as Dr. King?

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:50.110
Sure, some can gather
followers, but how tactful are

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:52.570
the causes for which they lead?

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:55.710
And what cause should
they be leading?

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:59.160
The question that I
pose before you today

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:03.380
is, to what or for
what should we lead?

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:06.330
To answer this question,
I started digging back,

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:10.340
back to the life of Dr.
King, for these answers.

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:13.700
I'm not quite sure if he
gave me a clear cut solution,

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:17.570
but he started opening up my
mind to some possible ones.

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:20.250
Allow me to share.

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:23.550
Let us talk about
purpose and cause.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.640
Many leaders are born with
the rightful talent to lead.

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:30.900
They may have great oratorical
skills, a beautiful, loud

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:34.650
voice, are persuasive
in speech, but does that

00:29:34.650 --> 00:29:36.630
make them a good leader?

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:37.590
No.

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:39.930
What makes them a
good leader is if they

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:42.330
choose a valid and
purposeful path

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:44.550
for those that
follow them, if they

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:47.340
choose not to lead them astray.

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:49.050
The first quality
of a good leader

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:52.770
is one who will never take
his eyes off the prize,

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:55.840
to make life better for others.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:59.370
Another important quality is
to be an exceptional learner,

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:03.990
to open one's eyes, mind,
and heart to the truth.

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:09.230
Do not be blindsided by
what is easy and fruitless.

00:30:09.230 --> 00:30:13.040
But beyond being an
exceptional learner and leader,

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.420
Dr. King was a teacher.

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:16.970
He made leaders.

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:19.700
He took his God-given talent
and stood at the pulpit

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:24.110
and said, people, are you
tired of bus segregation?

00:30:24.110 --> 00:30:25.820
Will you walk?

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:28.240
Well, they were so
tired of that bus

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.830
that thousands of citizens
of Montgomery, Alabama,

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:33.710
both black and white,
walked every day

00:30:33.710 --> 00:30:36.060
for more than six months.

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:39.060
But the truth remains
that they became leaders

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:41.580
when they followed Dr. King.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:43.710
They took their
energy and strength,

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:47.760
and got up, and started walking
with their two feet, and said,

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:51.060
we won't sit in
the back anymore.

00:30:51.060 --> 00:30:54.510
If I was to tell you to remember
anything today, I would say,

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:56.670
do not be that leader
that lets people

00:30:56.670 --> 00:30:59.160
sit in the back of the bus.

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:02.130
They deserve the front,
back, left side, right,

00:31:02.130 --> 00:31:05.040
whatever they desire.

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:07.970
This is the problem I see
that we need to resolve.

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:09.800
It is the problem
with leadership

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:14.410
that doesn't have the motivation
to build more leaders, to build

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:17.030
and sustain inclusion.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.520
What do I mean by inclusion?

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:22.230
It is allowing equal
opportunity for everyone

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:24.720
to achieve success.

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:27.080
Many view the illusion
of full inclusion

00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:31.100
is that glass ceiling that
no one can seem to break.

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:34.040
Well, I apologize
if I offend you.

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:36.380
Upon my reflection
of Dr. King's life,

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:39.260
I think it is quite
naive to believe

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:43.340
that one can break the glass
ceiling by his or herself.

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:45.800
Don't try to climb
to the top alone.

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:50.030
If you do, you will bump your
head and fall right back down.

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:52.610
We-- and I mean we--

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:55.070
need to work first
at teaching so

00:31:55.070 --> 00:31:57.830
that when we are
psychologically prepared

00:31:57.830 --> 00:32:00.530
to climb to the
highest peak together,

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:04.220
we will break
through any barriers.

00:32:04.220 --> 00:32:08.900
Remember, Dr. King taught that
inequality cannot be taken over

00:32:08.900 --> 00:32:10.790
by force alone.

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:14.190
We must first teach
the notion of equality.

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:17.420
Then and only then,
can we begin to talk

00:32:17.420 --> 00:32:20.630
about injustice and inequality.

00:32:20.630 --> 00:32:25.730
Then and only then, will the
illusion of total inclusion

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:30.440
transform into a clear
and tangible objective.

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:32.984
Thank you, and good morning.

00:32:32.984 --> 00:32:35.419
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:49.060 --> 00:32:50.760
GOMEZ: Thank you, Georgette.

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:52.789
We will now hear
from Eric Caulfield.

00:33:33.702 --> 00:33:34.702
CAULFIELD: Good morning.

00:33:34.702 --> 00:33:36.060
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:38.540
CAULFIELD: My name is
Russell Eric Caulfield,

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:40.550
and I'm a graduate
student in the Department

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:43.850
of Electrical Engineering
and Computer Science

00:33:43.850 --> 00:33:46.730
and a proud graduate of
Morehouse College of which

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:47.525
Dr.--

00:33:47.525 --> 00:33:52.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:54.915
--of Which Dr. King
was an alumnus.

00:33:58.170 --> 00:34:00.060
When I first got
the email saying

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:04.320
that I would get to speak at
the Martin Luther King Jr.

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:10.300
Breakfast, I was thrilled,
delighted, excited, elated,

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:14.042
and amused, ecstatic, overjoyed,
and completely enthused--

00:34:14.042 --> 00:34:16.808
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:20.550
--at the opportunity
to stand in front

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:23.880
of such an awesome and
diversely beautiful

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:26.679
gathering of human beings.

00:34:26.679 --> 00:34:28.989
And now, as I stand
here, it does my heart

00:34:28.989 --> 00:34:34.420
good to gaze out upon such a
celestial cornucopia of colors.

00:34:34.420 --> 00:34:36.755
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:44.710
A sublime symphony of hues, a
magnificently marvelous mosaic

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:48.520
of men and women of
different backgrounds, races,

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:51.120
and religions.

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:52.639
For in looking at
this group, one

00:34:52.639 --> 00:34:55.310
could be led to believe that
the quest of the man whom

00:34:55.310 --> 00:35:01.170
we commemorate on this morning
had indeed been completed.

00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:04.350
However, if one
were to take time

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:08.430
to ponder the preponderance
of perplexing peculiarities

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:12.120
persistently plaguing
people, then one

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:13.800
might come to the
conclusion that what

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:16.470
we are, in fact,
seeing is indeed

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:24.610
an illusion, more specifically,
the illusion of full inclusion.

00:35:24.610 --> 00:35:26.170
If visitors from
another world were

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:27.780
to drop by this
breakfast and peek in,

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:30.370
then they might think
that we here at MIT

00:35:30.370 --> 00:35:34.000
are indeed the
fulfillment of the dream.

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:37.300
But were they to visit the
lecture halls and office

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:41.080
buildings on a normal Friday
morning, what they would find

00:35:41.080 --> 00:35:44.050
is that there are less
than 150 African Americans

00:35:44.050 --> 00:35:49.230
in a graduate school of
nearly 6,000 students

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:52.650
and that there are less than 130
Hispanics in that same graduate

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:54.730
school.

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:57.220
More shockingly,
what they would find

00:35:57.220 --> 00:36:05.810
is that of the 486 full
tenured professors here at MIT,

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:08.920
only seven of them
are African American.

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:18.190
And of the 486 full tenured
professors here at MIT,

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:21.300
only six of them are Hispanic.

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:23.460
Granted, great
strides have been made

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:26.850
in terms of gender inclusion
at the undergraduate level,

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:29.500
though women make up less than
30% of the graduate school

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:32.370
and less than 20%
of the faculty.

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:34.650
What we have to
recognize is that issues

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:39.510
of gender and racial inclusion
are not mutually exclusive

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:41.970
but, instead, are complementary.

00:36:41.970 --> 00:36:45.540
For it's only when
we have men and women

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:48.660
of different backgrounds
working together can

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:53.190
we hope to achieve a full
range of new, insightful, and

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:55.558
innovative perspectives.

00:36:55.558 --> 00:36:58.028
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:10.150
But unfortunately, many of us
who grew up in a microwave,

00:37:10.150 --> 00:37:12.460
TV dinner, give-it-to-me-now,
I-want-it-yesterday type

00:37:12.460 --> 00:37:17.230
of society, expect that
inclusion will ride in like

00:37:17.230 --> 00:37:21.760
a handsome knight on a horse and
we sit back saying, inclusion,

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:27.026
inclusion, wherefore
art thou, inclusion?

00:37:27.026 --> 00:37:29.918
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:32.328 --> 00:37:34.400
But we have to
recognize that we are

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:36.920
wrestling against the
momentum of history,

00:37:36.920 --> 00:37:39.440
constantly spiraling downward,
plowing a path where,

00:37:39.440 --> 00:37:43.070
if unchecked, the
greedy, and ruthless,

00:37:43.070 --> 00:37:45.440
and short-sighted
tyrants of the world

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:50.050
might actually have their way.

00:37:50.050 --> 00:37:52.990
Ultimately, what we
have to recognize

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:57.820
is that the B boy from Brooklyn,
the chemist from Cambodia,

00:37:57.820 --> 00:38:01.390
the EE from Indonesia,
the MD from Mexico,

00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:04.870
the botanist from Botswana,
the mathematician from Morocco,

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:07.313
the ecologist from England,
the geologist from Germany,

00:38:07.313 --> 00:38:09.230
the poli sci from Poland,
and the psychiatrist

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:11.770
from Sri Lanka, the
architect from Argentina

00:38:11.770 --> 00:38:19.350
all bring to the table something
different and yet the same--

00:38:19.350 --> 00:38:24.980
competence and diversity.

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:26.570
They say that great
minds think alike,

00:38:26.570 --> 00:38:28.820
but I submit that truly
exceptional minds think

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:31.580
differently, for it is
our charge as sojourners

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:35.360
in this magnificent academic
mega mecca to think differently

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:38.930
and to grapple earnestly
with full inclusion.

00:38:38.930 --> 00:38:42.060
Chasing truth never has been
and never will be an easy task,

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:45.200
but we can keep in mind the
words of Arthur William Edgar

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:49.550
O'Shaughnessy who wrote,
"We are the music makers.

00:38:49.550 --> 00:38:51.500
And we are the
dreamers of dreams

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:53.210
wandering by the
lone sea breakers

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:55.010
and sitting by desolate streams.

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:57.590
The world-losers and the
world-forsakers on whom

00:38:57.590 --> 00:38:59.030
the pale moon gleams.

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:03.710
Yet we are the movers and
shakers of the world forever

00:39:03.710 --> 00:39:04.430
it seems."

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:07.520
In remembering Dr. King, we
should remember his work.

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:10.670
We must embrace difference,
press for equality,

00:39:10.670 --> 00:39:13.910
and break the rose-colored
glasses of sameness

00:39:13.910 --> 00:39:20.470
that prevent us from
seeing through the illusion

00:39:20.470 --> 00:39:24.240
of full inclusion.

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:30.640
So on this day of
remembrance, let

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:38.310
us not only commemorate
the man, but let

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:46.015
us also remember his mission.

00:39:46.015 --> 00:39:47.512
Thank you.

00:39:47.512 --> 00:39:50.007
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:15.672 --> 00:40:16.630
GOMEZ: Thank you, Eric.

00:40:16.630 --> 00:40:19.020
That was a wonderful speech.

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:21.210
Once again, I'd like to
thank both Eric Caulfield

00:40:21.210 --> 00:40:24.330
and Georgette Charles for
those wonderful remarks

00:40:24.330 --> 00:40:25.752
and for letting the words--

00:40:25.752 --> 00:40:27.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:34.608 --> 00:40:38.060
--and for letting the words of
Dr. King be heard once more.

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:40.010
I hope that everyone
here today has really

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:42.095
had a chance to listen
carefully to their words.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:47.490
Now I have the honor of
introducing Dean Robert P.

00:40:47.490 --> 00:40:55.021
Redwine, who will present the
2001 MLK Leadership Awards.

00:40:55.021 --> 00:40:58.472
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:10.785 --> 00:41:11.702
REDWINE: Good morning.

00:41:11.702 --> 00:41:13.430
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:15.410
REDWINE: I really don't
know why I always have

00:41:15.410 --> 00:41:17.220
to follow speeches like that.

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:19.100
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:26.290
But it is indeed an
honor and a privilege

00:41:26.290 --> 00:41:29.500
to be able to present this
year's Dr. Martin Luther King

00:41:29.500 --> 00:41:32.500
Jr. Leadership Awards
to four remarkable

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:34.750
and deserving individuals.

00:41:34.750 --> 00:41:37.180
As some of you know,
this happy task

00:41:37.180 --> 00:41:38.860
would normally have
been carried out

00:41:38.860 --> 00:41:42.640
by our Chancellor,
Professor Philip Clay.

00:41:42.640 --> 00:41:44.500
Phil is not able to
be with us today,

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:47.170
and I think I may
understand why now

00:41:47.170 --> 00:41:50.140
after he realized Eric would be
preceding him on the program.

00:41:50.140 --> 00:41:52.620
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:52.620 --> 00:41:56.460
But I am certainly pleased to
be able to serve in his absence.

00:41:56.460 --> 00:41:58.410
I will ask each
of the recipients

00:41:58.410 --> 00:42:02.430
to come forward one at a time
to receive his or her award.

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:05.130
After I offer some words
of congratulations,

00:42:05.130 --> 00:42:07.710
each recipient will then
have the opportunity

00:42:07.710 --> 00:42:10.260
to make a few remarks as well.

00:42:10.260 --> 00:42:14.884
I'd like first to ask Dr. Randal
Pinkett to come forward please.

00:42:14.884 --> 00:42:22.189
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:24.630 --> 00:42:26.970
Dr. Pinkett is an
alumnus of MIT,

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:31.650
having received the MBA, SM, and
PhD degrees from the Institute.

00:42:31.650 --> 00:42:35.070
He is also a graduate of
Rutgers University and a Rhodes

00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:36.430
Scholar.

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:38.760
He is currently chief
executive officer

00:42:38.760 --> 00:42:41.730
of Building Community with
Technology, a consulting

00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:44.940
firm in Plainfield, New Jersey.

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:48.150
Randal, those who nominated
you for this award

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:50.310
cited your an
extraordinary ability

00:42:50.310 --> 00:42:53.520
to inspire, encourage,
and lead others

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:55.590
as well the exceptional
ways that you

00:42:55.590 --> 00:42:57.960
have made significant
and lasting contributions

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:02.100
to the advancement of low income
people in urban neighborhoods.

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:04.590
While completing your
doctorate in the Media Lab, you

00:43:04.590 --> 00:43:06.930
and graduate colleague,
Richard O'Bryant

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:10.410
created a highly effective model
for bridging the digital divide

00:43:10.410 --> 00:43:12.720
by implementing a
computer network

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:15.660
in Camfield Estates of Roxbury.

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:17.490
This innovative
undertaking quickly

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:19.890
enhanced the neighborhood's
capacity for community

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:22.890
building and accessing
vital technology

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:25.380
and has drawn
national recognition.

00:43:25.380 --> 00:43:27.030
Your considerable
accomplishments

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:31.050
as a Rhodes Scholar, engineer,
community leader, researcher,

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:33.600
and entrepreneur
embodies so much

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:37.080
of what Dr. King desired
for all in our society.

00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:39.840
Your willingness to share
these talents with others

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:42.810
is not only admirable
but an exemplary way

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:44.790
of continuing his dream.

00:43:44.790 --> 00:43:47.880
It gives me great pleasure to
present to you this year's Dr.

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:50.688
Martin Luther King
Jr. Leadership Award.

00:43:50.688 --> 00:44:08.630
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:08.630 --> 00:44:11.120
PINKETT: I want thank you for
the wonderful presentation

00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:13.880
of the award and also
giving honor to God

00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:15.505
who is at the head of my life.

00:44:15.505 --> 00:44:16.605
I also--

00:44:16.605 --> 00:44:17.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:17.810 --> 00:44:18.310
Yes.

00:44:21.640 --> 00:44:25.690
It's actually here in Boston
that I reclaimed my faith

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:29.345
at Morningstar Baptist Church
after two years abroad.

00:44:29.345 --> 00:44:30.970
Coming back to the
Lord and coming back

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:33.640
to the family of God has
been truly a blessing for me.

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:36.820
And I continue to reap
the benefits of having

00:44:36.820 --> 00:44:39.860
rejoined the Body of Christ.

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:44.740
I want to first
give a shout out,

00:44:44.740 --> 00:44:46.630
so to speak, to
Georgette and to Eric

00:44:46.630 --> 00:44:48.550
for the wonderful
remarks they gave

00:44:48.550 --> 00:44:50.300
on the reflections of Dr. King.

00:44:50.300 --> 00:44:52.200
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:55.050 --> 00:44:59.370
I was, I was, I'm
so proud and floored

00:44:59.370 --> 00:45:02.380
at both of your remarks.

00:45:02.380 --> 00:45:04.800
I want to just briefly
reflect on our theme

00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:08.670
this morning, which is
from dreams to reality.

00:45:08.670 --> 00:45:14.100
Many look at the way we talk
about Dr. King as a dreamer

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:17.880
and argue or sometimes
want to suggest that

00:45:17.880 --> 00:45:21.510
by framing him in that
way that Dr. King may

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:24.240
have had his head in the
clouds or was unrealistic

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:25.290
in his expectations.

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:28.230
Or you kind of begin to
fantasize that he didn't really

00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:31.380
have a handle on the issues
because we constantly make

00:45:31.380 --> 00:45:33.060
reference to the
man-- he had a dream--

00:45:33.060 --> 00:45:34.360
and the dream.

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:36.780
But I believe when
you really cut

00:45:36.780 --> 00:45:39.870
to the core of what that
means, that to really effect

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:43.080
change in society,
to really find

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:47.970
ways to transform our day-to-day
reality, you have to be,

00:45:47.970 --> 00:45:50.310
in some respects, a dreamer.

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:52.020
You have to believe
that in some way

00:45:52.020 --> 00:45:55.650
you are able to affect or
create a new reality that you

00:45:55.650 --> 00:45:59.490
don't see physically or within
your everyday experience.

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:02.230
I've heard people say
faith is an acronym that

00:46:02.230 --> 00:46:04.800
stands Feeling As
If Things Happened,

00:46:04.800 --> 00:46:06.330
actually believing
that something

00:46:06.330 --> 00:46:08.920
has come to manifest itself.

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:12.450
And when we look at
children, for example,

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:17.730
and the childlike qualities
that young people have where

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:21.600
they almost suspend reality and
believe that they can do almost

00:46:21.600 --> 00:46:25.200
anything, I believe Dr.
King retained something

00:46:25.200 --> 00:46:27.240
about that quality, that
he believed somewhere

00:46:27.240 --> 00:46:29.490
within himself that
he could do anything,

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:31.320
that he could see a
world that we see today

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:33.360
or that we're working
toward seeing today,

00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:34.800
as Eric mentioned.

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:39.640
That was a new
manifestation of inclusion.

00:46:39.640 --> 00:46:44.240
And I think about
the environment

00:46:44.240 --> 00:46:45.590
that he must have worked within.

00:46:45.590 --> 00:46:48.048
There had to have been people
around him that said, Martin,

00:46:48.048 --> 00:46:48.703
you're crazy.

00:46:48.703 --> 00:46:50.120
Martin, to think
that you're going

00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:51.500
to be able to make
the kind of changes

00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:52.958
that you want to
be able to make,

00:46:52.958 --> 00:46:54.500
you're not going to
be able to do it.

00:46:54.500 --> 00:46:57.400
But somehow, in some way, he
was able to move beyond that,

00:46:57.400 --> 00:47:01.400
to move beyond that and hold
onto that childlike quality,

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:03.750
again, that helps us to
believe we can do anything.

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:05.600
And so I stand at
this podium today

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:07.370
realizing that I never
would have imagined

00:47:07.370 --> 00:47:10.700
that I would have received a
Rhodes Scholarship or three

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:13.790
degrees from MIT or
a Martin Luther King

00:47:13.790 --> 00:47:15.385
Award for Leadership at MIT.

00:47:15.385 --> 00:47:16.760
And I'm honored,
and I'm blessed,

00:47:16.760 --> 00:47:21.147
and I'm humbled that MIT
has chosen to recognize me

00:47:21.147 --> 00:47:22.230
for what I've done so far.

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:23.730
And I hope that I'm
able to continue

00:47:23.730 --> 00:47:25.770
to do good work moving forward.

00:47:25.770 --> 00:47:28.040
And I just want to
end by recognizing

00:47:28.040 --> 00:47:30.498
some of those that are here
this morning that have come out

00:47:30.498 --> 00:47:31.885
to share in this honor.

00:47:31.885 --> 00:47:33.260
First, my mom,
Elizabeth Pinkett,

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:35.284
who came here up from
New Jersey along with me.

00:47:35.284 --> 00:47:35.784
Mom.

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:44.550
I want to thank her.

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:45.750
My father is deceased.

00:47:45.750 --> 00:47:47.400
And I want to thank
my mom for being

00:47:47.400 --> 00:47:49.920
the strength and the
guiding force in my life

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:52.200
and for really instilling
the values in me

00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:54.090
and continuing to be a
support there for me.

00:47:54.090 --> 00:47:54.923
And I love you, Mom.

00:47:54.923 --> 00:47:56.912
And I appreciate you for that.

00:47:56.912 --> 00:47:59.370
I want to also recognize my
advisor, Mitchel Resnick, who's

00:47:59.370 --> 00:48:00.050
here somewhere--

00:48:00.050 --> 00:48:01.530
Mitchel, just raise your hand--

00:48:01.530 --> 00:48:04.947
who's been very supportive in
my doctoral work here at MIT.

00:48:04.947 --> 00:48:07.821
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:09.153 --> 00:48:10.070
And just a few others.

00:48:10.070 --> 00:48:12.730
Ceasar McDowell, who's here, a
member of my thesis committee,

00:48:12.730 --> 00:48:15.810
Richard O'Bryant, who was
mentioned in my bio, who

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:18.720
helped me out with the
product over in Roxbury,

00:48:18.720 --> 00:48:21.990
the family of people of color
here at MIT, Chris Jones

00:48:21.990 --> 00:48:24.410
and Robin and Deans
Osgood and Colbert,

00:48:24.410 --> 00:48:27.332
and Melissa McDaniels,
Dean Charles, Dean Staton,

00:48:27.332 --> 00:48:28.540
Brian [INAUDIBLE], all those.

00:48:28.540 --> 00:48:29.910
And last but not
least, I want to thank

00:48:29.910 --> 00:48:32.100
the residents of Camfield, where
I've worked for the past three

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:32.790
years.

00:48:32.790 --> 00:48:35.008
I'm working with them to
look at ways technology can

00:48:35.008 --> 00:48:36.300
improve low income communities.

00:48:36.300 --> 00:48:39.330
Nakia Keizer, Paulette Ford,
Thaddeus Miles, Wayne Williams,

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:41.627
Donna Fisher, Garfield,
Luis, and the whole board.

00:48:41.627 --> 00:48:42.460
Thank you very much.

00:48:42.460 --> 00:48:43.607
God bless all of you.

00:48:43.607 --> 00:48:46.589
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:58.020 --> 00:48:59.790
REDWINE: Thank you, Randal.

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:01.740
Next, I would like to
ask Sergeant Cheryl

00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:05.500
Vossmer to come forward please.

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:08.428
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:17.492
Sergeant Vossmer has been an
MIT police officer for 17 years.

00:49:17.492 --> 00:49:19.950
She has been a longtime member
of the President's Committee

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:23.360
on Campus Race Relations and
has demonstrated in many ways

00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:25.710
a commitment to better
communication and understanding

00:49:25.710 --> 00:49:28.640
between MIT's
various communities.

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:31.680
Cheryl, those who
nominated you cited

00:49:31.680 --> 00:49:34.650
your longstanding commitment to
maintaining the values of Dr.

00:49:34.650 --> 00:49:38.170
King by enthusiastically
serving the students, staff,

00:49:38.170 --> 00:49:42.210
and faculty of MIT and many
residents of greater Cambridge.

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:44.070
You consider the
successful attainment

00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:47.400
of a good and livable community
as part of your job and work

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:51.150
hard to keep this ideal in
the forefront of your mind.

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:53.340
Whether you are assisting
a troubled student,

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:55.890
participating in World
AIDS Day activities,

00:49:55.890 --> 00:49:58.920
considering an issue before
the Campus Committee on Race

00:49:58.920 --> 00:50:02.340
Relations, or collecting toys
for disadvantaged children,

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:05.370
you are known as a thoughtful
and compassionate professional

00:50:05.370 --> 00:50:08.220
who takes her responsibilities
very seriously.

00:50:08.220 --> 00:50:11.550
Your philosophy
and actions embody

00:50:11.550 --> 00:50:14.070
Dr. King's forever
challenging question,

00:50:14.070 --> 00:50:16.440
what are you doing for others?

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:19.590
It gives me great pleasure to
present to you this year's Dr.

00:50:19.590 --> 00:50:22.720
Martin Luther King
Jr. Leadership Award.

00:50:22.720 --> 00:50:25.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:37.608 --> 00:50:38.400
VOSSMER: Thank you.

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:45.250
I'm a little bit
shorter, so I have

00:50:45.250 --> 00:50:46.980
to roll with this a little bit.

00:50:46.980 --> 00:50:51.880
But I just want to
say thank you so very

00:50:51.880 --> 00:50:55.170
much for this prestigious honor.

00:50:55.170 --> 00:50:58.890
It's difficult for me to be
recognized for doing the things

00:50:58.890 --> 00:51:01.510
that I love to do.

00:51:01.510 --> 00:51:04.730
I'm very humbled, and thank you.

00:51:04.730 --> 00:51:07.040
As you all know, I also have
a difficult time standing

00:51:07.040 --> 00:51:08.450
behind a podium.

00:51:08.450 --> 00:51:12.290
I'd much rather be out walking
around with all of you.

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:16.580
But the Reverend Zina Jacque--

00:51:16.580 --> 00:51:19.340
she's from Boston--
she told a story which

00:51:19.340 --> 00:51:21.310
had a wonderful effect on me.

00:51:21.310 --> 00:51:25.860
And I want to share
just a bit with you.

00:51:25.860 --> 00:51:29.920
Many of you will be
very familiar with it.

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:33.790
It was about a small,
young boy named David

00:51:33.790 --> 00:51:41.060
and how he took one smooth rock
to slay a giant named Goliath.

00:51:41.060 --> 00:51:45.270
David went on to become
a king, a leader.

00:51:45.270 --> 00:51:47.210
Some of you may have
picked up the rocks

00:51:47.210 --> 00:51:50.340
that you found on
your table today.

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:53.010
Carry it with you.

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:58.540
Let it be a reminder
that you can take on

00:51:58.540 --> 00:52:01.780
more than you think
that you can handle.

00:52:01.780 --> 00:52:09.490
Because it only takes one
smooth rock to slay a giant.

00:52:09.490 --> 00:52:11.110
A few weeks ago, I
had the privilege

00:52:11.110 --> 00:52:13.960
to travel to Ground
Zero in New York City

00:52:13.960 --> 00:52:17.390
and volunteer with
others from MIT.

00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:18.890
And I'd like to
read a letter to you

00:52:18.890 --> 00:52:22.340
from a sixth grader from
the Roger Park Middle School

00:52:22.340 --> 00:52:24.910
in Danbury, Connecticut.

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:29.490
She writes, "Dear World
Trade Center rescue workers,

00:52:29.490 --> 00:52:31.890
I feel you're doing a good job.

00:52:31.890 --> 00:52:35.710
I thank you for spending
your time to help.

00:52:35.710 --> 00:52:39.500
I also want to thank you
for sacrificing your health.

00:52:39.500 --> 00:52:43.400
You have made a
difference in America.

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:44.960
God bless America.

00:52:44.960 --> 00:52:47.640
Your friend, Kirsten."

00:52:47.640 --> 00:52:50.490
I share this letter with you
because, as this young lady

00:52:50.490 --> 00:52:55.890
points out, you have made a
difference to all of America.

00:52:55.890 --> 00:53:01.430
You, too, can make a
difference in the world.

00:53:01.430 --> 00:53:04.810
And as Dr. Martin
Luther, Jr., said,

00:53:04.810 --> 00:53:07.930
"Life's most persistent
and urgent question

00:53:07.930 --> 00:53:11.260
is, what are you
doing for others?"

00:53:14.627 --> 00:53:17.690
In our workaday lives, we are
filled with the opportunities

00:53:17.690 --> 00:53:19.830
to bless others.

00:53:19.830 --> 00:53:24.720
The power of a single glance
or an encouraging smile

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:28.400
must never be underestimated.

00:53:28.400 --> 00:53:32.670
As Dr. Martin Luther
King, again, he said,

00:53:32.670 --> 00:53:37.030
our most urgent question is,
what are you doing for others.

00:53:37.030 --> 00:53:39.120
So in closing, let
me say, whatever

00:53:39.120 --> 00:53:43.230
your ideas, whatever your
goals or commitments are,

00:53:43.230 --> 00:53:47.140
your leadership must
come from your heart.

00:53:47.140 --> 00:53:50.810
My heart I give to you.

00:53:50.810 --> 00:53:53.120
I know how grateful I am.

00:53:53.120 --> 00:53:55.860
My attitude's of gratitude.

00:53:55.860 --> 00:53:57.540
And I'm grateful
that so many people

00:53:57.540 --> 00:54:00.810
care to make a
difference to overcome

00:54:00.810 --> 00:54:04.380
the evil acts of a few.

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:09.480
Believe in yourself,
and ask yourself,

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:12.640
what are you doing for others?

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:15.570
Thank you very much.

00:54:15.570 --> 00:54:18.100
You've all made a
difference in my life.

00:54:18.100 --> 00:54:20.550
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:38.710 --> 00:54:41.080
REDWINE: Thank you, Cheryl.

00:54:41.080 --> 00:54:45.500
Next, I ask Professor Paul
Gray to come forward please.

00:54:45.500 --> 00:54:47.950
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:55.800 --> 00:54:58.650
Professor Gray is, of course,
very well-known to the MIT

00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:01.570
community, having served in
a number of important roles,

00:55:01.570 --> 00:55:05.940
including as president of the
Institute from 1980 to 1990.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:09.690
He, in fact, was one of those
who started in MIT's annual MLK

00:55:09.690 --> 00:55:12.150
celebration in 1977.

00:55:12.150 --> 00:55:14.040
He is currently an
active faculty member

00:55:14.040 --> 00:55:16.500
in the Department of Electrical
Engineering and Computer

00:55:16.500 --> 00:55:18.150
Science.

00:55:18.150 --> 00:55:21.900
Paul, those who nominated
you for this award

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:25.140
cited your steadfast
and enduring commitment

00:55:25.140 --> 00:55:27.540
as a faculty member and
senior administrator

00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:30.570
to providing equal opportunities
and higher education

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:32.490
for people of color.

00:55:32.490 --> 00:55:36.060
As chair of the Task Force on
Educational Opportunity first

00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:41.160
convened in September 1968, you
took on an unprecedented role,

00:55:41.160 --> 00:55:43.350
developing institutional
policies that

00:55:43.350 --> 00:55:46.920
led to the admission of 42
African American students

00:55:46.920 --> 00:55:48.900
by the next academic year.

00:55:48.900 --> 00:55:51.450
Those who witnessed that
era can now more fully

00:55:51.450 --> 00:55:55.080
appreciate how daunting it
was for all men and women

00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:57.060
to lead equitably
and responsibly

00:55:57.060 --> 00:55:59.490
during a time of great change.

00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:02.310
However, because of a
far-reaching vision and ability

00:56:02.310 --> 00:56:04.610
to stand firmly by
your principles,

00:56:04.610 --> 00:56:07.410
you frequently and
effectively have advocated

00:56:07.410 --> 00:56:09.270
for greater inclusion.

00:56:09.270 --> 00:56:12.060
Through your sponsorship of
the first national conference

00:56:12.060 --> 00:56:14.190
on issues facing
black administrators

00:56:14.190 --> 00:56:17.250
at predominantly white
colleges and universities held

00:56:17.250 --> 00:56:21.350
on this campus in 1982 and your
appointment of Dean Shirley

00:56:21.350 --> 00:56:23.490
McBay to the
Academic Council, you

00:56:23.490 --> 00:56:25.650
have demonstrated an
unrelenting desire

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:28.650
to foster a clearer
understanding and awareness

00:56:28.650 --> 00:56:30.960
of racial issues
in our community.

00:56:30.960 --> 00:56:33.550
As a compassionate
advisor and mentor,

00:56:33.550 --> 00:56:36.120
you have tirelessly guided
students and professionals

00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:38.530
to greater levels
of achievement.

00:56:38.530 --> 00:56:41.280
Your more than 40 years of
service on behalf of others

00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:44.340
embodies the values
and ideals of Dr. King.

00:56:44.340 --> 00:56:46.560
Your efforts have
helped many who still

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:49.170
want to sustain his dream.

00:56:49.170 --> 00:56:52.560
It gives me great pleasure to
present to you this year's Dr.

00:56:52.560 --> 00:56:55.423
Martin Luther King
Jr. Leadership Award.

00:56:55.423 --> 00:56:58.381
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:12.190 --> 00:57:13.570
GRAY: Thank you.

00:57:13.570 --> 00:57:19.420
And my heartfelt thanks
to the planning committee,

00:57:19.420 --> 00:57:22.675
to all who were involved in
making this day possible.

00:57:25.630 --> 00:57:33.840
I came to MIT in the watershed
year of the 20th century, 1950.

00:57:33.840 --> 00:57:38.220
And with a little time
out for military service,

00:57:38.220 --> 00:57:43.530
I've been involved now at MIT
in a variety of capacities

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:46.450
for just 50 years.

00:57:46.450 --> 00:57:52.980
It is clear to me that
MIT, at the opening

00:57:52.980 --> 00:58:00.300
of the 21st century, is a far
richer, warmer, more humane

00:58:00.300 --> 00:58:04.600
place than it was 50 years ago.

00:58:04.600 --> 00:58:09.660
And it is so because of the
presence in significant numbers

00:58:09.660 --> 00:58:13.560
of people of color,
of Hispanic people,

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:17.040
of women, of Native Americans.

00:58:17.040 --> 00:58:19.420
MIT has changed.

00:58:19.420 --> 00:58:22.510
People in this room are
a part of that change.

00:58:22.510 --> 00:58:27.080
And it has made a
genuine difference.

00:58:27.080 --> 00:58:29.450
Now Eric Caulfield is right.

00:58:29.450 --> 00:58:33.870
There is much that
remains to be done.

00:58:33.870 --> 00:58:39.080
We have taken first steps
on that road from the dream

00:58:39.080 --> 00:58:40.900
to reality.

00:58:40.900 --> 00:58:42.440
And there remain
many steps still

00:58:42.440 --> 00:58:47.210
to be taken to achieve
the outcome of which he

00:58:47.210 --> 00:58:51.200
spoke so eloquently.

00:58:51.200 --> 00:58:53.780
I felt privileged
and grateful to have

00:58:53.780 --> 00:58:57.260
had the opportunity
in the late 1960s

00:58:57.260 --> 00:59:00.890
to work with people such
as Shirley Jackson, Jim

00:59:00.890 --> 00:59:06.080
Turner, John Mims,
Al Hill, to take

00:59:06.080 --> 00:59:12.660
those first steps toward making
MIT a more inclusive place.

00:59:12.660 --> 00:59:15.030
In the years since then,
I've had the opportunity

00:59:15.030 --> 00:59:17.010
to work with many others--

00:59:17.010 --> 00:59:24.760
my friend Clarence Williams,
Jerry Friedman, Leo Osgood,

00:59:24.760 --> 00:59:27.640
Mike Colbert, and many others.

00:59:27.640 --> 00:59:30.370
It is the effort--

00:59:30.370 --> 00:59:33.690
the steady, persistent effort--

00:59:33.690 --> 00:59:37.860
of individuals who are
committed to moving along

00:59:37.860 --> 00:59:43.500
the path from that dream to
a new reality that have made

00:59:43.500 --> 00:59:47.610
and will continue to make at
MIT a difference in the nature

00:59:47.610 --> 00:59:52.800
of this community,
will continue to push

00:59:52.800 --> 00:59:57.960
this institution, this very
special place, along the road

00:59:57.960 --> 01:00:03.660
to full participation in the
entire community of this nation

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:09.250
and of this world as it
will be in the 21st century.

01:00:09.250 --> 01:00:10.871
Thank you all very much.

01:00:10.871 --> 01:00:13.326
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:23.160 --> 01:00:25.700
REDWINE: Thank you, Paul.

01:00:25.700 --> 01:00:29.996
Next, I ask Ms. Tamara Williams
to come forward please.

01:00:29.996 --> 01:00:38.690
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:38.690 --> 01:00:40.177
Ms. Williams is a
graduate student

01:00:40.177 --> 01:00:42.510
in the Department of Electrical
Engineering and Computer

01:00:42.510 --> 01:00:43.670
Science.

01:00:43.670 --> 01:00:47.540
She is a 1998 graduate of
Tennessee State University.

01:00:47.540 --> 01:00:50.270
At MIT, she has been a
leader and several on-campus

01:00:50.270 --> 01:00:51.510
organizations.

01:00:51.510 --> 01:00:53.870
She has also volunteered
for Tutoring Plus

01:00:53.870 --> 01:00:56.510
and has worked with Cambridge
public school students

01:00:56.510 --> 01:00:59.170
in mathematics and English.

01:00:59.170 --> 01:01:02.320
Tamara, those who nominated
you for this award

01:01:02.320 --> 01:01:04.930
cited your range of
enriching contributions

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:06.520
to the MIT community.

01:01:06.520 --> 01:01:09.160
While undertaking doctoral
studies at the Artificial

01:01:09.160 --> 01:01:11.710
Intelligence Laboratory
you have committed

01:01:11.710 --> 01:01:14.200
to serving as Co-chair of
the Black Graduate Student

01:01:14.200 --> 01:01:17.110
Association and of
Color Creations.

01:01:17.110 --> 01:01:19.330
Your creative leadership
of these organizations

01:01:19.330 --> 01:01:22.210
has motivated and enabled
other graduate students

01:01:22.210 --> 01:01:25.300
to attend the National Society
of Black Engineers National

01:01:25.300 --> 01:01:27.760
Convention where they
shared information

01:01:27.760 --> 01:01:31.030
about their disciplines and
encouraged potential applicants

01:01:31.030 --> 01:01:34.420
to consider further studies
in science and technology.

01:01:34.420 --> 01:01:37.240
You have also reached out to
fellow student organizations

01:01:37.240 --> 01:01:40.360
by coordinating monthly dinners
and developing networking

01:01:40.360 --> 01:01:43.480
opportunities for individuals
and groups concerned

01:01:43.480 --> 01:01:46.210
with issues that
affect women of color.

01:01:46.210 --> 01:01:48.370
As a volunteer in
Tutoring Plus, you

01:01:48.370 --> 01:01:52.130
assisted Cambridge's
elementary, middle school,

01:01:52.130 --> 01:01:54.850
and high school students with
their mathematics and language

01:01:54.850 --> 01:01:56.080
arts subjects.

01:01:56.080 --> 01:01:58.300
You have distinguished
yourself as a contributor

01:01:58.300 --> 01:02:00.610
to the quality of
life on this campus,

01:02:00.610 --> 01:02:02.470
and your efforts
honorably represent

01:02:02.470 --> 01:02:05.960
what Dr. King expected
of himself and of others.

01:02:05.960 --> 01:02:09.050
Your recognition is a fitting
tribute to his memory.

01:02:09.050 --> 01:02:10.870
It is with great
pleasure that I present

01:02:10.870 --> 01:02:14.410
to you this year's Dr. Martin
Luther King Jr. Leadership

01:02:14.410 --> 01:02:15.732
Award.

01:02:15.732 --> 01:02:18.708
[APPLAUSE]

01:02:28.275 --> 01:02:29.650
WILLIAMS: Good
morning, everyone.

01:02:29.650 --> 01:02:31.090
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

01:02:31.090 --> 01:02:35.350
WILLIAMS: First, I'd like to
thank Marilyn Pierce and Peggy

01:02:35.350 --> 01:02:37.720
Carney of the Electrical
Engineering Department

01:02:37.720 --> 01:02:40.780
for nominating me
for this award.

01:02:40.780 --> 01:02:43.510
I'd also like to thank
the leadership committee

01:02:43.510 --> 01:02:47.890
for deeming me worthy to receive
such a great honor because I

01:02:47.890 --> 01:02:52.240
am indeed humbled,
and I feel great.

01:02:52.240 --> 01:02:55.870
I'd like to pose a
question to you all.

01:02:55.870 --> 01:02:59.950
That question is, how do
you measure your success?

01:02:59.950 --> 01:03:03.730
Do you measure it by how
many degrees you have?

01:03:03.730 --> 01:03:06.970
How much money you make?

01:03:06.970 --> 01:03:08.080
How many cars you have?

01:03:08.080 --> 01:03:10.860
What type of house you live in?

01:03:10.860 --> 01:03:14.970
Well, some time ago in my
lifetime, I read a quote--

01:03:14.970 --> 01:03:18.850
and I can't remember who
actually said this statement.

01:03:18.850 --> 01:03:23.310
However, it has really helped
me with my outlook on life.

01:03:23.310 --> 01:03:27.780
And the quote is,
"measure your success

01:03:27.780 --> 01:03:32.320
based on how many people
you make successful."

01:03:32.320 --> 01:03:36.670
Can you imagine if the faculty,
the staff, the students,

01:03:36.670 --> 01:03:39.880
the citizens, you
citizens here today,

01:03:39.880 --> 01:03:43.750
if you had that outlook
on life and when

01:03:43.750 --> 01:03:46.480
thinking about yourself,
you actually took

01:03:46.480 --> 01:03:50.160
a step back and said,
is a little time

01:03:50.160 --> 01:03:52.260
out of my day contributing?

01:03:52.260 --> 01:03:55.440
Saying hi to someone,
giving someone a warm smile,

01:03:55.440 --> 01:03:57.900
or even helping someone
with their schoolwork,

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:00.420
or talking to someone
about their problems--

01:04:00.420 --> 01:04:03.550
how beneficial is that
going to be to that person?

01:04:03.550 --> 01:04:05.250
But most importantly,
even to yourself,

01:04:05.250 --> 01:04:07.740
it's very rewarding
to give to others.

01:04:07.740 --> 01:04:11.440
Because in the end, you're
really helping yourself.

01:04:11.440 --> 01:04:16.470
And I just want everyone here
to just leave with that thought.

01:04:16.470 --> 01:04:19.320
Measure your success
based on how many people

01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:20.950
you makes successful.

01:04:20.950 --> 01:04:24.070
And I guarantee the world
will be a very better place--

01:04:24.070 --> 01:04:24.916
a better place.

01:04:24.916 --> 01:04:26.404
Thank you.

01:04:26.404 --> 01:04:28.884
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:39.175 --> 01:04:40.300
REDWINE: Thank you, Tamara.

01:04:40.300 --> 01:04:42.940
And I'd just like to
congratulate all four winners

01:04:42.940 --> 01:04:43.520
again.

01:04:43.520 --> 01:04:44.828
Congratulations.

01:04:44.828 --> 01:04:47.298
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:52.845 --> 01:04:53.970
GOMEZ: Thank you very much.

01:04:53.970 --> 01:04:55.803
And again, congratulations
to the recipients

01:04:55.803 --> 01:04:57.170
of all the awards.

01:04:57.170 --> 01:05:00.260
I'd like to take a
moment to give you

01:05:00.260 --> 01:05:03.060
a little announcement about
the MLK seminar installation.

01:05:03.060 --> 01:05:05.060
And I'll read a little
bit of this brochure that

01:05:05.060 --> 01:05:07.700
was available at the front.

01:05:07.700 --> 01:05:11.450
"Since January 1999, students
in the Martin Luther King Junior

01:05:11.450 --> 01:05:15.650
IAP Design Seminar have created
installations in Lobby 10

01:05:15.650 --> 01:05:18.650
to depict the struggles and
accomplishments of African

01:05:18.650 --> 01:05:22.640
Americans and other people
of color throughout history.

01:05:22.640 --> 01:05:24.470
This year's centerpiece
demonstrates

01:05:24.470 --> 01:05:26.990
the theme of the MLK
breakfast speaker,

01:05:26.990 --> 01:05:28.490
From Dreams to Reality--

01:05:28.490 --> 01:05:31.070
The Illusion of Full Inclusion.

01:05:31.070 --> 01:05:34.040
From afar, you see a
circle of mannequins.

01:05:34.040 --> 01:05:35.780
As you are invited
to join in the circle

01:05:35.780 --> 01:05:37.790
by standing in the
middle, you realize

01:05:37.790 --> 01:05:40.430
that none of the mannequins
are looking toward you.

01:05:40.430 --> 01:05:42.290
Instead, they have
their backs to you,

01:05:42.290 --> 01:05:45.510
and you are standing
on the word minority."

01:05:45.510 --> 01:05:47.750
Just a little preview,
and I invite all of you

01:05:47.750 --> 01:05:51.730
to go to Lobby 10 and take
a look at the installation.

01:05:51.730 --> 01:05:53.430
And now, we have
another selection

01:05:53.430 --> 01:05:56.460
by the Cambridge Rindge
and Latin High School choir

01:05:56.460 --> 01:05:58.170
led by Mr. Donald Patterson.

