01:11:48.458 --> 01:11:51.000
GOMEZ: Thank you again to the
Cambridge Rindge and Latin High

01:11:51.000 --> 01:11:52.830
School choir.

01:11:52.830 --> 01:11:54.930
Now President Vest
will give some remarks

01:11:54.930 --> 01:11:58.036
and proceed to introduce our
keynote speaker, Mr. Tavis

01:11:58.036 --> 01:11:59.988
Smiley.

01:11:59.988 --> 01:12:02.916
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:12.200 --> 01:12:13.430
VEST: Thank you, Mirabel.

01:12:13.430 --> 01:12:15.110
And good morning, everybody.

01:12:15.110 --> 01:12:16.900
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

01:12:16.900 --> 01:12:19.030
VEST: Thank you
all for coming out

01:12:19.030 --> 01:12:22.330
this morning to take part
in this great annual MIT

01:12:22.330 --> 01:12:23.860
tradition.

01:12:23.860 --> 01:12:29.910
It's always a real highlight
of Becky's and my year.

01:12:29.910 --> 01:12:34.050
But Eric, I have
to tell you, when

01:12:34.050 --> 01:12:39.030
I got the email asking me
to come here this morning, I

01:12:39.030 --> 01:12:45.150
knew, yet again, I would be
absolutely humbled by people

01:12:45.150 --> 01:12:49.650
like Georgette and Eric,
by their rhetorical skills,

01:12:49.650 --> 01:12:55.090
of course, but also by the
clarity of their thought,

01:12:55.090 --> 01:12:57.890
the steadfastness
of their heart,

01:12:57.890 --> 01:13:01.620
their focus on the cause.

01:13:01.620 --> 01:13:04.680
I'm especially pleased that
we are joined this morning

01:13:04.680 --> 01:13:07.620
by many friends and colleagues
from the wider Cambridge

01:13:07.620 --> 01:13:10.590
community, our community.

01:13:10.590 --> 01:13:13.740
We are all citizens of this
great city on the banks

01:13:13.740 --> 01:13:15.450
of the Charles River.

01:13:15.450 --> 01:13:18.300
Cambridge is our common ground.

01:13:18.300 --> 01:13:21.630
We work hard to make it a
vital and sustaining community

01:13:21.630 --> 01:13:22.890
for all.

01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:25.590
And it is fitting
that we come together

01:13:25.590 --> 01:13:28.100
to celebrate this morning.

01:13:28.100 --> 01:13:32.280
And so I would like to extend
special welcomes to City

01:13:32.280 --> 01:13:34.458
Councilor Denise Simmons.

01:13:34.458 --> 01:13:41.010
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:41.010 --> 01:13:43.050
State Representative
Paul DeMarcus

01:13:43.050 --> 01:13:46.410
was with us through most of
the morning but had to leave,

01:13:46.410 --> 01:13:49.530
so we thank him in absentia.

01:13:49.530 --> 01:13:52.650
Chairman Benjamin Barnes
from the Cambridge Licensing

01:13:52.650 --> 01:13:53.638
Commission.

01:13:53.638 --> 01:13:59.080
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:59.080 --> 01:14:01.660
Melvina Monteiro,
Executive Director

01:14:01.660 --> 01:14:04.494
of the Cambridge Police
Review Advisory Board.

01:14:04.494 --> 01:14:09.140
[APPLAUSE]

01:14:09.140 --> 01:14:14.920
Thank you all very, very much
for joining us this morning.

01:14:14.920 --> 01:14:17.170
One year ago at
this gathering, I

01:14:17.170 --> 01:14:22.110
noted that 2001
was an iconic date,

01:14:22.110 --> 01:14:25.550
the year, as everybody
at MIT knows,

01:14:25.550 --> 01:14:29.650
of Arthur C. Clarke's
Space Odyssey.

01:14:29.650 --> 01:14:32.200
Arthur C. Clarke
and Stanley Kubrick

01:14:32.200 --> 01:14:35.680
imagined that 2001
would be remembered

01:14:35.680 --> 01:14:39.450
as a year in which computers
became nearly human

01:14:39.450 --> 01:14:42.130
and in which everyday
passengers would

01:14:42.130 --> 01:14:46.870
board spacecraft
and drift gracefully

01:14:46.870 --> 01:14:49.840
toward a colony on the moon.

01:14:49.840 --> 01:14:55.150
In reality, this past
year will be remembered

01:14:55.150 --> 01:15:01.180
as the time when everyday
passengers boarded aircraft

01:15:01.180 --> 01:15:04.397
not five miles from
where we sit this morning

01:15:04.397 --> 01:15:08.500
and pierced our
proudest buildings,

01:15:08.500 --> 01:15:13.270
ending the lives of more than
3,000 innocent people going

01:15:13.270 --> 01:15:16.890
about their daily business.

01:15:16.890 --> 01:15:20.560
We discovered some
elemental truths--

01:15:20.560 --> 01:15:27.140
that evil is bred by ignorance,
and poverty, and absolutism,

01:15:27.140 --> 01:15:29.900
that our own technology
can be turned

01:15:29.900 --> 01:15:36.180
against us by the crudest
actions of determined people.

01:15:36.180 --> 01:15:39.180
At the same time, the memory
of that terrible reality

01:15:39.180 --> 01:15:42.960
will always conjure in
our minds the sounds

01:15:42.960 --> 01:15:49.730
of people of every color,
creed, and corner of our country

01:15:49.730 --> 01:15:54.630
singing America the
Beautiful together.

01:15:54.630 --> 01:15:56.930
It has been a dark time,
and it has cried out

01:15:56.930 --> 01:15:59.510
for new understandings.

01:15:59.510 --> 01:16:01.820
A month after the attacks,
The New York Times

01:16:01.820 --> 01:16:08.930
ran a story whose headline was
"Attack Narrows Racial Divide."

01:16:08.930 --> 01:16:12.800
Listen to an excerpt
from that story.

01:16:12.800 --> 01:16:15.530
"Ever so slightly, the
attacks on the Trade Center

01:16:15.530 --> 01:16:20.720
have tweaked the city's
traditional racial divides.

01:16:20.720 --> 01:16:22.880
Some of it is how
ordinary men and women

01:16:22.880 --> 01:16:25.640
react to each other on
the streets, on subways,

01:16:25.640 --> 01:16:27.260
and bodegas.

01:16:27.260 --> 01:16:29.630
Some people attribute
it to the solemnity

01:16:29.630 --> 01:16:33.350
that hangs over the
city, others to fear.

01:16:33.350 --> 01:16:38.570
Still others to a new
found unity as Americans.

01:16:38.570 --> 01:16:42.440
Whatever it is, the way that
New Yorkers perceive one another

01:16:42.440 --> 01:16:46.640
across color lines, however
accurate those perceptions were

01:16:46.640 --> 01:16:50.280
to begin with, has changed."

01:16:50.280 --> 01:16:53.980
And in December, the
Tampa, Florida Tribune

01:16:53.980 --> 01:16:56.470
reported on interviews
with two dozen people

01:16:56.470 --> 01:17:00.190
about race relations,
noting that,

01:17:00.190 --> 01:17:02.890
"maybe it's a
veneer, as some say.

01:17:02.890 --> 01:17:05.150
Maybe there's something deeper.

01:17:05.150 --> 01:17:07.480
But after the attacks,
some black Americans

01:17:07.480 --> 01:17:11.410
noticed that whites
talked to them more.

01:17:11.410 --> 01:17:15.100
Others felt less vulnerable
to racial profiling.

01:17:15.100 --> 01:17:17.800
Knowing that suspicion
had found a new target,

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:20.630
they struggled with
their emotions."

01:17:20.630 --> 01:17:24.730
It is undeniable that crisis
and fear drew all Americans

01:17:24.730 --> 01:17:27.820
together as never before.

01:17:27.820 --> 01:17:33.220
In the face of adversity,
we build bridges.

01:17:33.220 --> 01:17:38.770
My question this morning is,
why can we not bridge divides

01:17:38.770 --> 01:17:42.100
in the face of opportunity?

01:17:42.100 --> 01:17:47.260
First, historians point out that
this phenomenon of sudden unity

01:17:47.260 --> 01:17:53.560
and bridge building is
observed frequently in wartime

01:17:53.560 --> 01:17:57.750
but that it should not
be expected to last long.

01:17:57.750 --> 01:18:00.750
And indeed, just a couple of
weeks after the New York Times

01:18:00.750 --> 01:18:03.750
article, The Boston
Globe reported

01:18:03.750 --> 01:18:07.170
that many said the
shared tragedy had quote,

01:18:07.170 --> 01:18:10.980
"briefly bridged
Boston's racial divide."

01:18:10.980 --> 01:18:13.360
Briefly.

01:18:13.360 --> 01:18:16.020
And listen again to the
statement from the Tampa

01:18:16.020 --> 01:18:18.420
Tribune, that African
Americans they interviewed

01:18:18.420 --> 01:18:22.170
felt some relief over improved
relationships with whites

01:18:22.170 --> 01:18:26.040
but struggled with their
emotions knowing that,

01:18:26.040 --> 01:18:29.950
"suspicion had
found a new target."

01:18:29.950 --> 01:18:34.600
Therein lies a
huge warning sign.

01:18:34.600 --> 01:18:38.380
Our nation, indeed, is
at war with terrorism.

01:18:38.380 --> 01:18:41.860
And we must take unusual
care to ensure the safety

01:18:41.860 --> 01:18:45.100
and security of our land
and of all the people

01:18:45.100 --> 01:18:47.230
who dwell within it.

01:18:47.230 --> 01:18:52.480
But we also must
remain an open society.

01:18:52.480 --> 01:18:57.670
And we surely must
maintain open universities.

01:18:57.670 --> 01:19:02.900
Without openness, there
can be no inclusion.

01:19:02.900 --> 01:19:07.440
And openness means openness not
only across American society,

01:19:07.440 --> 01:19:11.240
but also openness to serious and
talented students and scholars

01:19:11.240 --> 01:19:14.360
who come to our shores
from other countries

01:19:14.360 --> 01:19:17.190
to build a new America.

01:19:17.190 --> 01:19:21.990
MIT is very proud of
our Nobel Prize winners.

01:19:21.990 --> 01:19:25.850
Those laureates who currently
are active in our midst

01:19:25.850 --> 01:19:33.480
were born in the United
States, Italy, Germany, Mexico,

01:19:33.480 --> 01:19:36.720
Japan, and India.

01:19:36.720 --> 01:19:38.850
No more than that
need be said to show

01:19:38.850 --> 01:19:42.540
why we must guard the grand
tradition of welcoming those

01:19:42.540 --> 01:19:45.120
who come here from every
corner of the world

01:19:45.120 --> 01:19:50.430
to learn and to advance our
institutions and our country.

01:19:50.430 --> 01:19:52.590
But even as we
pursue the principle

01:19:52.590 --> 01:19:57.000
of international openness,
we know full well

01:19:57.000 --> 01:20:02.580
that race remains a deeply
troubling issue in America.

01:20:02.580 --> 01:20:05.340
We must be unwavering
in our quest

01:20:05.340 --> 01:20:09.550
to eradicate this
reality from our society.

01:20:09.550 --> 01:20:13.500
We must accelerate the
movement of inclusion

01:20:13.500 --> 01:20:17.790
from illusion to reality.

01:20:17.790 --> 01:20:20.220
So here are two
particular challenges

01:20:20.220 --> 01:20:22.740
for all of us today--

01:20:22.740 --> 01:20:25.650
not only to fight for
gains in mutual respect

01:20:25.650 --> 01:20:29.190
and understanding,
but to sustain them,

01:20:29.190 --> 01:20:33.000
to make sure that they are not
brief, sentimental aberrations

01:20:33.000 --> 01:20:38.670
but permanent improvements
of heart and habit,

01:20:38.670 --> 01:20:42.360
and to understand
that if all we achieve

01:20:42.360 --> 01:20:45.990
is to broaden the
definition of "us"

01:20:45.990 --> 01:20:48.630
while still indulging
our desires to believe

01:20:48.630 --> 01:20:53.220
in some broad, sinister
"them," we have not really

01:20:53.220 --> 01:20:57.870
grown any closer to
what Dr. King called

01:20:57.870 --> 01:21:00.760
the beloved community.

01:21:00.760 --> 01:21:05.750
Now "community" is a
word worth pausing over.

01:21:05.750 --> 01:21:10.770
None of us may be prepared
to define it precisely.

01:21:10.770 --> 01:21:14.360
But I believe that within
the larger family of MIT,

01:21:14.360 --> 01:21:18.590
community is a concept that
people understand, think

01:21:18.590 --> 01:21:25.762
about, and value these days in
a way we may not have before.

01:21:25.762 --> 01:21:28.390
And I believe that
was increasingly true

01:21:28.390 --> 01:21:33.760
even before September 11, 2001.

01:21:33.760 --> 01:21:35.980
Several years ago,
our Task Force

01:21:35.980 --> 01:21:37.660
on Student Life
and Learning put us

01:21:37.660 --> 01:21:39.790
on a new path of
building community

01:21:39.790 --> 01:21:43.740
into the educational
experience of our students.

01:21:43.740 --> 01:21:47.340
And we have seen it begin to
take hold among our students,

01:21:47.340 --> 01:21:51.120
the design of our new
buildings, and new programs

01:21:51.120 --> 01:21:54.500
inside and outside
our classrooms.

01:21:54.500 --> 01:21:56.450
These commitments
and new opportunities

01:21:56.450 --> 01:22:00.160
should mean a true
living together,

01:22:00.160 --> 01:22:02.960
a true working
together, regardless

01:22:02.960 --> 01:22:08.060
of race, class, culture, age,
field of study, religion,

01:22:08.060 --> 01:22:10.640
or experience.

01:22:10.640 --> 01:22:14.480
I wish, as Eric pointed out,
that I could say that that were

01:22:14.480 --> 01:22:18.860
uniformly the case at MIT,
but of course, I can't.

01:22:18.860 --> 01:22:20.930
Not yet.

01:22:20.930 --> 01:22:22.700
We've made progress
over the years,

01:22:22.700 --> 01:22:24.590
but we need to
rededicate ourselves

01:22:24.590 --> 01:22:28.520
to the principles of
openness and inclusion

01:22:28.520 --> 01:22:31.940
if we are to have
a real community.

01:22:31.940 --> 01:22:35.930
The fact remains that most
American adults live largely

01:22:35.930 --> 01:22:38.240
segregated lives.

01:22:38.240 --> 01:22:41.960
Our workplaces are
increasingly mixed,

01:22:41.960 --> 01:22:45.890
but our neighborhoods
typically are not.

01:22:45.890 --> 01:22:49.780
For white students especially,
their years here at MIT

01:22:49.780 --> 01:22:52.900
may, in fact, be the most
integrated experience

01:22:52.900 --> 01:22:55.570
of their entire life.

01:22:55.570 --> 01:22:59.190
And yet, we have much
to do if our students--

01:22:59.190 --> 01:23:01.080
all of our students--

01:23:01.080 --> 01:23:03.750
are to have the real benefits
of living and studying

01:23:03.750 --> 01:23:08.100
in a truly multicultural
and multiracial community.

01:23:08.100 --> 01:23:11.820
This begins with
admissions and access.

01:23:11.820 --> 01:23:15.060
If we are to provide the kind
of environment and education

01:23:15.060 --> 01:23:19.200
that our students
deserve, we must reach out

01:23:19.200 --> 01:23:23.190
and be open to all those
who will best contribute

01:23:23.190 --> 01:23:27.000
to and benefit from MIT.

01:23:27.000 --> 01:23:32.590
Without access, there
is no inclusion.

01:23:32.590 --> 01:23:36.790
We will-- and let there be no
misunderstanding about this--

01:23:36.790 --> 01:23:40.120
we will continue our commitment
to affirmative action

01:23:40.120 --> 01:23:41.530
in admissions.

01:23:41.530 --> 01:23:44.500
We will continue to follow the
tenets of the Bakke decision,

01:23:44.500 --> 01:23:48.160
and we will continue to do the
hard work necessary to recruit

01:23:48.160 --> 01:23:55.030
to MIT extraordinarily talented
African Americans, Latinos,

01:23:55.030 --> 01:23:59.160
Native Americans, and
other students of color.

01:23:59.160 --> 01:24:02.870
But admitting a richly
diverse class each year,

01:24:02.870 --> 01:24:07.350
as has been said today,
is just the beginning.

01:24:07.350 --> 01:24:11.300
We have an opportunity and,
I believe, an obligation

01:24:11.300 --> 01:24:15.110
to make the MIT experience
as positive, constructive,

01:24:15.110 --> 01:24:17.570
and transformative
as possible for each

01:24:17.570 --> 01:24:23.650
and every one of our students
individually and collectively.

01:24:23.650 --> 01:24:27.070
We truly must get
beyond the illusion

01:24:27.070 --> 01:24:31.770
and on to the reality
of full inclusion.

01:24:31.770 --> 01:24:35.530
It will take time, and goodwill,
and hard work, and faith.

01:24:35.530 --> 01:24:38.440
But I know that it can be
done, that we will reach

01:24:38.440 --> 01:24:40.865
the place where we
can meet each other

01:24:40.865 --> 01:24:44.930
and know each other
simply as human beings,

01:24:44.930 --> 01:24:49.720
not as distant
representatives of some group.

01:24:49.720 --> 01:24:54.580
And when we do, we will have
found the most important route

01:24:54.580 --> 01:24:59.540
to mutual understanding
and true equality.

01:24:59.540 --> 01:25:02.900
Despite our
frustrations, I continue

01:25:02.900 --> 01:25:05.960
to be an optimist,
perhaps because I

01:25:05.960 --> 01:25:08.540
see so many bright
signs close by here

01:25:08.540 --> 01:25:13.430
in our own community starting
with our four award winners

01:25:13.430 --> 01:25:15.490
this morning.

01:25:15.490 --> 01:25:18.670
And another undeniably bright
sign has been here with us,

01:25:18.670 --> 01:25:22.780
too, in the person
of Tavis Smiley.

01:25:22.780 --> 01:25:25.690
As you probably know,
Tavis has launched

01:25:25.690 --> 01:25:29.050
a new daily audio magazine
on National Public

01:25:29.050 --> 01:25:33.720
Radio geared to an
African American audience.

01:25:33.720 --> 01:25:37.290
But that was only after
he had established himself

01:25:37.290 --> 01:25:41.525
as a wildly popular institution
in a dozen other ways

01:25:41.525 --> 01:25:45.570
with a long running
program on BET,

01:25:45.570 --> 01:25:48.630
as a frequent commentator
on several different radio

01:25:48.630 --> 01:25:53.020
and television networks, as
the author of five books,

01:25:53.020 --> 01:25:58.220
including How To Make
Black America Better.

01:25:58.220 --> 01:26:01.790
But I must say that my
favorite fact about Tavis

01:26:01.790 --> 01:26:04.160
is that he is currently
running symposiums

01:26:04.160 --> 01:26:08.090
across the country designed to
promote the use of technology

01:26:08.090 --> 01:26:11.090
within the African
American community.

01:26:11.090 --> 01:26:16.160
As he puts it, the
subject is e-inclusion,

01:26:16.160 --> 01:26:19.740
making a digital difference.

01:26:19.740 --> 01:26:23.760
E-inclusion, openness, and
opportunity across our nation

01:26:23.760 --> 01:26:28.230
and around the world are things
that are very much on minds

01:26:28.230 --> 01:26:32.730
here at MIT as we
work to make together

01:26:32.730 --> 01:26:36.570
to make virtually all of
our educational materials

01:26:36.570 --> 01:26:40.230
freely available to
everyone in the world

01:26:40.230 --> 01:26:42.960
through our
OpenCourseWare project.

01:26:42.960 --> 01:26:44.940
But enough about us.

01:26:44.940 --> 01:26:47.430
Ladies and gentlemen,
it's my great pleasure

01:26:47.430 --> 01:26:49.920
to introduce Mr. Tavis Smiley.

01:26:49.920 --> 01:26:53.420
[APPLAUSE]

01:27:05.920 --> 01:27:07.670
SMILEY: Thank you.

01:27:07.670 --> 01:27:09.670
Y'all better sit down, I
ain't said nothing yet.

01:27:12.280 --> 01:27:14.955
You might want that back
in about 20 minutes.

01:27:14.955 --> 01:27:17.580
I always laugh at folk who give
standing ovations before you've

01:27:17.580 --> 01:27:18.480
ever said anything.

01:27:18.480 --> 01:27:21.155
You may disagree with
everything I had to say.

01:27:21.155 --> 01:27:22.530
Why did I stand
up for that Negro

01:27:22.530 --> 01:27:24.870
before he ever said something?

01:27:24.870 --> 01:27:27.450
I'm delighted to be in
Cambridge and to be back

01:27:27.450 --> 01:27:28.950
in the great state
of Massachusetts.

01:27:28.950 --> 01:27:33.750
Let me commence by thanking
your president, Mr. Vest,

01:27:33.750 --> 01:27:35.790
and his wife for
their fine hospitality

01:27:35.790 --> 01:27:38.370
and for being our
tablemates this morning.

01:27:38.370 --> 01:27:41.473
We had good conversation,
delighted to sit with them.

01:27:41.473 --> 01:27:43.140
I'm also honored to
be at the same table

01:27:43.140 --> 01:27:45.060
with your immediate
past president, Dr.

01:27:45.060 --> 01:27:46.980
Gray, and his wife.

01:27:46.980 --> 01:27:49.800
And so it's a good feeling
to go somewhere and have

01:27:49.800 --> 01:27:52.820
two presidents show up
to greet you at a table.

01:27:52.820 --> 01:27:54.570
I must have done
something right, I guess,

01:27:54.570 --> 01:27:57.820
or been lucky for the
right table to sit at.

01:27:57.820 --> 01:27:59.230
So thank you for being here.

01:27:59.230 --> 01:28:01.397
I think it is very important.

01:28:01.397 --> 01:28:02.980
It's always important
for me at least,

01:28:02.980 --> 01:28:06.030
and it makes an impression on
me when you show up somewhere

01:28:06.030 --> 01:28:07.860
at a celebration such
as this and the folk

01:28:07.860 --> 01:28:10.920
who are in charge, who happen
not to be African American,

01:28:10.920 --> 01:28:14.440
deem it important and necessary
to be there themselves.

01:28:14.440 --> 01:28:17.040
And so I'm always
honored to see persons

01:28:17.040 --> 01:28:18.960
who are in charge of
making decisions who

01:28:18.960 --> 01:28:21.735
value these issues, as
evidenced by their presence

01:28:21.735 --> 01:28:23.610
and indeed by their
strong words this morning

01:28:23.610 --> 01:28:27.108
understand that the real
essence of diversity

01:28:27.108 --> 01:28:28.650
is something that
has to be addressed

01:28:28.650 --> 01:28:31.320
and that diversity and
tolerance of not just words

01:28:31.320 --> 01:28:32.820
but indeed ways of
life that we have

01:28:32.820 --> 01:28:37.450
to be more serious about making
our reality on a daily basis.

01:28:37.450 --> 01:28:39.310
Let me also, before I
get into my remarks,

01:28:39.310 --> 01:28:43.080
thank the co-chairs of this
event, Mr. Feld and Mr. Osgood,

01:28:43.080 --> 01:28:45.390
for the invitation and
for the hard work that you

01:28:45.390 --> 01:28:50.280
and all the committee have done
to make this event possible.

01:28:50.280 --> 01:28:51.670
Wonderful turnout obviously.

01:28:51.670 --> 01:28:54.280
You couldn't get any more
people in here if you wanted to,

01:28:54.280 --> 01:28:55.810
so it's a great turnout.

01:28:55.810 --> 01:28:58.870
I think, as has already
been said, it does in fact,

01:28:58.870 --> 01:29:00.810
as you look around the
room from this podium,

01:29:00.810 --> 01:29:02.560
now I get a chance to
see what everybody's

01:29:02.560 --> 01:29:05.160
been talking about how
beautiful the room really looks

01:29:05.160 --> 01:29:09.900
and how this room really
does speak to the fact that--

01:29:09.900 --> 01:29:11.940
this room, quite frankly,
represents a microcosm

01:29:11.940 --> 01:29:13.830
of what the world really is.

01:29:13.830 --> 01:29:15.630
And these are the
kinds of gatherings,

01:29:15.630 --> 01:29:17.100
it seems to me,
that we ought to be

01:29:17.100 --> 01:29:19.740
a part of when we talk
about celebrating the life

01:29:19.740 --> 01:29:25.092
and legacy of one Dr.
Martin Luther King, Jr.

01:29:25.092 --> 01:29:26.550
So much has been
said this morning.

01:29:26.550 --> 01:29:30.090
I quite frankly have
said two or three times

01:29:30.090 --> 01:29:33.760
to people sitting around me,
I don't know why I'm here.

01:29:33.760 --> 01:29:36.720
I mean everything that needed to
be said, has already been said.

01:29:36.720 --> 01:29:37.830
It has been said.

01:29:37.830 --> 01:29:39.060
It has been sung.

01:29:39.060 --> 01:29:41.850
It has been-- you know.

01:29:41.850 --> 01:29:44.820
I don't know what else y'all
expected me to come with this

01:29:44.820 --> 01:29:46.370
morning.

01:29:46.370 --> 01:29:52.500
I told Brother Osgood, if you
ever set me up like this again,

01:29:52.500 --> 01:29:53.980
put me up at the
end of a program

01:29:53.980 --> 01:29:57.840
when everything has been said,
behind two presidents, two

01:29:57.840 --> 01:30:00.660
or three choirs, Eric--

01:30:00.660 --> 01:30:04.027
[LAUGHTER]

01:30:10.290 --> 01:30:12.038
--and then 45 minutes
behind schedule.

01:30:12.038 --> 01:30:13.580
And I'm supposed to
hit a grand slam.

01:30:13.580 --> 01:30:15.960
Right.

01:30:15.960 --> 01:30:16.680
I've been set up.

01:30:16.680 --> 01:30:17.970
It's a setup.

01:30:17.970 --> 01:30:19.890
I've been bamboozled,
hoodwinked, run amok,

01:30:19.890 --> 01:30:20.565
and led astray.

01:30:24.780 --> 01:30:26.190
I am mindful.

01:30:26.190 --> 01:30:27.987
I am mindful of the time.

01:30:27.987 --> 01:30:29.820
We are running a little
bit behind schedule,

01:30:29.820 --> 01:30:31.620
so let me offer some
abbreviated remarks

01:30:31.620 --> 01:30:33.600
if I might, but
remarks, I hope, that

01:30:33.600 --> 01:30:36.707
would nonetheless cause us to
think as we go along our way.

01:30:36.707 --> 01:30:38.790
You'll be happy to know,
as a member of the debate

01:30:38.790 --> 01:30:41.640
team at Indiana University,
that my debate coach taught

01:30:41.640 --> 01:30:44.580
me a couple of very important
lessons about public speaking.

01:30:44.580 --> 01:30:47.000
The first she
called the four S's.

01:30:47.000 --> 01:30:47.550
The four S's.

01:30:47.550 --> 01:30:51.480
Always remember, Tavis, had to
stand up, speak up, shut up,

01:30:51.480 --> 01:30:54.270
and sit your behind down.

01:30:54.270 --> 01:30:56.280
Stand up, speak up,
shut up, and sit down.

01:30:56.280 --> 01:30:59.190
And she said, secondly, that
a few minutes-- just a few

01:30:59.190 --> 01:31:03.690
minutes-- is long enough for a
good presentation and too long

01:31:03.690 --> 01:31:05.160
for a bad one.

01:31:05.160 --> 01:31:07.890
A few minutes is long enough
for a good presentation

01:31:07.890 --> 01:31:09.510
and too long for a bad one.

01:31:09.510 --> 01:31:12.052
Now if you're black-- and I see
that some of y'all up in here

01:31:12.052 --> 01:31:13.790
this morning are--

01:31:13.790 --> 01:31:15.730
that's a black thing
right there, ain't it?

01:31:15.730 --> 01:31:19.410
Why black folk always got
to be up in something?

01:31:19.410 --> 01:31:20.760
Who started that?

01:31:20.760 --> 01:31:23.240
Up in MIT.

01:31:23.240 --> 01:31:24.370
Up in the barber shop.

01:31:24.370 --> 01:31:26.310
Up in the beauty salon.

01:31:26.310 --> 01:31:27.660
Up in folk business.

01:31:27.660 --> 01:31:28.560
We always up in.

01:31:28.560 --> 01:31:29.685
I don't where that started.

01:31:32.068 --> 01:31:33.610
I'm glad you up in
here this morning.

01:31:33.610 --> 01:31:36.420
But the point, the point
that I want to make

01:31:36.420 --> 01:31:39.150
is that if you're black and
you've been in a church house

01:31:39.150 --> 01:31:41.660
before on a Sunday morning, you
know what I'm talking about.

01:31:41.660 --> 01:31:45.750
That a few minutes is long
enough for a good presentation

01:31:45.750 --> 01:31:48.345
and too long for a bad one.

01:31:48.345 --> 01:31:50.220
Last Sunday, at my own
church in Los Angeles,

01:31:50.220 --> 01:31:54.433
the reverend was doing good
at about the 22-minute mark.

01:31:54.433 --> 01:31:56.100
And then he stretched
it a little longer

01:31:56.100 --> 01:31:57.428
than he should have.

01:31:57.428 --> 01:31:59.220
And by the time he got
around to opening up

01:31:59.220 --> 01:32:04.260
the doors of the church,
wasn't nobody up in there

01:32:04.260 --> 01:32:06.510
because he didn't know how
to stand up, speak up, shut

01:32:06.510 --> 01:32:08.498
up, and sit down.

01:32:08.498 --> 01:32:11.040
I hope, though, in the time that
I have left here to be good,

01:32:11.040 --> 01:32:14.940
to be quick, and to be gone,
so that y'all can get on with

01:32:14.940 --> 01:32:16.260
the rest of your day.

01:32:16.260 --> 01:32:18.270
It seems to me, though,
right quick here,

01:32:18.270 --> 01:32:22.080
when you talk about Dr. King in
terms of celebrating his life

01:32:22.080 --> 01:32:25.860
and his legacy, the real
essence of King's life,

01:32:25.860 --> 01:32:28.290
as far as I'm concerned,
is challenging

01:32:28.290 --> 01:32:30.580
each and every one of us.

01:32:30.580 --> 01:32:36.940
I believe I can put it this way,
to reexamine our assumptions.

01:32:36.940 --> 01:32:39.420
That's my thesis, and
I'm sticking with it.

01:32:39.420 --> 01:32:42.870
That King's life and legacy
and his work, at the very core,

01:32:42.870 --> 01:32:45.060
at its very essence,
was about challenging

01:32:45.060 --> 01:32:50.730
each and every one of us to
examine, to indeed reexamine,

01:32:50.730 --> 01:32:52.650
our assumptions.

01:32:52.650 --> 01:32:55.140
To reexamine our assumptions.

01:32:55.140 --> 01:32:57.660
And everything that King did,
everything that he worked for,

01:32:57.660 --> 01:33:00.330
everything that he labored
for, every word that he wrote,

01:33:00.330 --> 01:33:03.330
every bit of prose
that he uttered

01:33:03.330 --> 01:33:05.040
was all dedicated
to the proposition,

01:33:05.040 --> 01:33:07.930
it seems to me, to challenge
each and every one of us,

01:33:07.930 --> 01:33:11.910
no matter our station in life,
to reexamine the assumptions,

01:33:11.910 --> 01:33:17.630
assumptions about this country,
assumptions about its people,

01:33:17.630 --> 01:33:20.240
indeed assumptions
about ourselves.

01:33:20.240 --> 01:33:23.280
The life and legacy of King
is about challenging us,

01:33:23.280 --> 01:33:26.280
it seems to me, to
reexamine the assumptions.

01:33:26.280 --> 01:33:28.833
Now, I've got to lay
this out as my foundation

01:33:28.833 --> 01:33:30.500
because if you can't
get with that, then

01:33:30.500 --> 01:33:32.042
you can't get with
the rest of what I

01:33:32.042 --> 01:33:33.620
want to offer you this morning.

01:33:33.620 --> 01:33:36.560
I hope that we can all agree
at least that King's life is

01:33:36.560 --> 01:33:40.520
about challenging us to
reexamine our assumptions,

01:33:40.520 --> 01:33:44.965
to reexamine those things
that we believe to be true.

01:33:44.965 --> 01:33:47.590
There is one thing this morning,
it seems to me, that all of us

01:33:47.590 --> 01:33:49.960
have to agree upon,
I hope, beyond that.

01:33:49.960 --> 01:33:55.030
And that is that this moment,
today, is a defining moment

01:33:55.030 --> 01:33:57.580
in the life of this country.

01:33:57.580 --> 01:33:58.790
This is a defining moment.

01:33:58.790 --> 01:34:03.340
There have been very few who
have bounded up on this podium

01:34:03.340 --> 01:34:06.490
this morning, have stood
behind this lectern,

01:34:06.490 --> 01:34:08.140
and have not made
some reference--

01:34:08.140 --> 01:34:10.400
either directly or in
some oblique manner--

01:34:10.400 --> 01:34:13.535
some reference to
September 11 of last year.

01:34:13.535 --> 01:34:15.910
For those who are on the
lecture circuit, those who are--

01:34:15.910 --> 01:34:18.368
not just on the lecture circuit,
for any of us, all of us--

01:34:18.368 --> 01:34:21.670
it is impossible these
days to have conversation

01:34:21.670 --> 01:34:23.590
without having
September 11 come up

01:34:23.590 --> 01:34:25.822
at some point as a reference.

01:34:25.822 --> 01:34:27.280
Now I'm bothered
by those of us who

01:34:27.280 --> 01:34:30.610
look at everything through
the prism of September 11.

01:34:30.610 --> 01:34:32.410
I mean, there's a
line here that we

01:34:32.410 --> 01:34:34.030
need to be careful
about not crossing.

01:34:34.030 --> 01:34:36.070
We cannot look at everything.

01:34:36.070 --> 01:34:39.790
Every decision we make cannot be
through the prism of September

01:34:39.790 --> 01:34:40.330
11.

01:34:40.330 --> 01:34:46.130
You do understand that there
was life before September 11.

01:34:46.130 --> 01:34:49.370
And even though I'm getting
less and less confident

01:34:49.370 --> 01:34:54.350
of this, Mr. Bush, after we
hunt him down and smoke him out,

01:34:54.350 --> 01:34:59.510
after we find him dead or alive,
once we know for certain where

01:34:59.510 --> 01:35:02.700
Mr. Bin Laden is and we
get al-Qaeda under control,

01:35:02.700 --> 01:35:04.605
life we will go on.

01:35:04.605 --> 01:35:05.480
That's the good news.

01:35:05.480 --> 01:35:07.070
There was life
before September 11,

01:35:07.070 --> 01:35:11.000
and there will be life after
we get this terrorism situation

01:35:11.000 --> 01:35:12.860
under some better control.

01:35:12.860 --> 01:35:16.100
And so it behooves each of
us not to look at everything,

01:35:16.100 --> 01:35:19.100
and not to make every decision,
and not to alter our lives

01:35:19.100 --> 01:35:23.450
in every possible way merely
through the prism of September

01:35:23.450 --> 01:35:24.810
11.

01:35:24.810 --> 01:35:27.560
But having said that, who
in this room could deny?

01:35:27.560 --> 01:35:33.600
Who can deny that this is, for
America, a defining moment?

01:35:33.600 --> 01:35:37.320
This King celebration
in the year 2002

01:35:37.320 --> 01:35:40.800
as Mr. President said a
moment ago is like none other.

01:35:40.800 --> 01:35:41.760
Things have changed.

01:35:41.760 --> 01:35:42.635
Things are different.

01:35:42.635 --> 01:35:45.070
Our lives have been
altered in ways,

01:35:45.070 --> 01:35:47.220
I think, quite frankly
forever, but ways

01:35:47.220 --> 01:35:51.250
that we don't even yet know
how dramatically our lives have

01:35:51.250 --> 01:35:52.720
been altered.

01:35:52.720 --> 01:35:54.640
We don't even know all of that.

01:35:54.640 --> 01:35:57.460
The whole has not been told
yet of how dramatically

01:35:57.460 --> 01:35:58.630
our lives have been altered.

01:35:58.630 --> 01:36:00.700
But we all must
agree, it seems to me,

01:36:00.700 --> 01:36:05.285
that this is a defining
moment in America.

01:36:05.285 --> 01:36:06.497
You still with me?

01:36:06.497 --> 01:36:07.080
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:36:07.080 --> 01:36:07.770
SMILEY: All right.

01:36:07.770 --> 01:36:08.820
So here's the question for me.

01:36:08.820 --> 01:36:10.390
Since everybody's
asking questions,

01:36:10.390 --> 01:36:12.770
let me ask a question.

01:36:12.770 --> 01:36:16.138
And particularly since I'm
talking to an audience at MIT--

01:36:16.138 --> 01:36:17.430
you're much brighter than I am.

01:36:17.430 --> 01:36:21.600
I can't tell you nothing anyway,
so let me ask you a few things.

01:36:21.600 --> 01:36:22.252
I can do that.

01:36:22.252 --> 01:36:23.460
I'm good at asking questions.

01:36:23.460 --> 01:36:25.450
I do that for a living
every single day.

01:36:25.450 --> 01:36:29.260
But far be it for me to try
to tell you all anything.

01:36:29.260 --> 01:36:32.400
It seems to me that if
this is a defining moment,

01:36:32.400 --> 01:36:34.440
then the question
is begged today

01:36:34.440 --> 01:36:41.520
as to how we take this defining
moment and redefine America.

01:36:41.520 --> 01:36:43.410
I lost somebody.

01:36:43.410 --> 01:36:46.520
I didn't come all the
way from LA to Cambridge

01:36:46.520 --> 01:36:49.640
to just talk about this
being a defining moment,

01:36:49.640 --> 01:36:51.530
to simply and merely
celebrate the life

01:36:51.530 --> 01:36:52.880
and legacy of Dr. King.

01:36:52.880 --> 01:36:54.560
I could have done
that in Los Angeles.

01:36:54.560 --> 01:36:59.060
I came here because you
are uniquely positioned

01:36:59.060 --> 01:37:03.950
as the best and the brightest
that this country has to offer.

01:37:03.950 --> 01:37:06.890
Those who will move from
this place to become leaders

01:37:06.890 --> 01:37:10.340
in all kinds of institutions,
in corporate America,

01:37:10.340 --> 01:37:15.110
in government, and beyond,
you are uniquely and best

01:37:15.110 --> 01:37:19.430
positioned to not just
raise the question of how

01:37:19.430 --> 01:37:23.960
we take this defining moment and
redefine America, but, indeed,

01:37:23.960 --> 01:37:27.800
to go about the business
of answering that question.

01:37:27.800 --> 01:37:29.330
Let me submit something to you.

01:37:29.330 --> 01:37:33.120
It would be a travesty,
for me, for this country

01:37:33.120 --> 01:37:36.420
to have endured what
we endured on the day

01:37:36.420 --> 01:37:40.080
that those diabolical attacks
were levied against the World

01:37:40.080 --> 01:37:41.850
Trade Center and the Pentagon.

01:37:41.850 --> 01:37:44.610
It would be beyond horrific.

01:37:44.610 --> 01:37:46.820
It is, quite
frankly, intolerable.

01:37:46.820 --> 01:37:49.210
It is unbelievable.

01:37:49.210 --> 01:37:52.080
It is unacceptable,
untenable, quite frankly,

01:37:52.080 --> 01:37:55.490
to imagine that after what
we endured on September 11

01:37:55.490 --> 01:37:59.430
with planes taking off
from Logan Airport,

01:37:59.430 --> 01:38:02.720
that those of you
in this edifice

01:38:02.720 --> 01:38:07.100
would allow that day
to come and to go,

01:38:07.100 --> 01:38:10.700
to understand that it is
a defining moment for us,

01:38:10.700 --> 01:38:15.890
and to not do anything about
taking this defining moment

01:38:15.890 --> 01:38:18.650
and redefining America.

01:38:18.650 --> 01:38:23.450
Shame on us if we conclude and
agree that this is a defining

01:38:23.450 --> 01:38:25.700
moment for our country
and that those of us

01:38:25.700 --> 01:38:29.630
who are the gifted,
those of us who have

01:38:29.630 --> 01:38:31.577
been blessed beyond measure--

01:38:31.577 --> 01:38:33.660
the Bible puts it this
way, to whom much is given,

01:38:33.660 --> 01:38:35.120
much is required.

01:38:35.120 --> 01:38:38.290
To whom much is given,
much is expected.

01:38:38.290 --> 01:38:40.390
If not you, then who?

01:38:40.390 --> 01:38:43.420
Somebody has to ask
the tough questions.

01:38:43.420 --> 01:38:44.980
Somebody has got
to raise the issue

01:38:44.980 --> 01:38:50.960
of how we take this defining
moment and redefine America.

01:38:50.960 --> 01:38:53.220
I want to submit
that to you today

01:38:53.220 --> 01:38:56.630
as perhaps one of the
most propitious questions

01:38:56.630 --> 01:38:58.520
that we ought to
be asking ourselves

01:38:58.520 --> 01:39:01.110
at this juncture in
this country's history.

01:39:01.110 --> 01:39:05.930
How do we take this defining
moment and redefine America?

01:39:05.930 --> 01:39:08.480
Now, one would think that
that has already happened.

01:39:08.480 --> 01:39:11.750
Indeed, as I've listened
to folk behind this podium

01:39:11.750 --> 01:39:13.850
this morning, there
is some evidence.

01:39:13.850 --> 01:39:16.250
The president spoke,
President Vest

01:39:16.250 --> 01:39:18.950
spoke, rather wonderfully
to this notion,

01:39:18.950 --> 01:39:21.470
as did others who stood up
behind this podium moments

01:39:21.470 --> 01:39:22.827
earlier.

01:39:22.827 --> 01:39:24.410
Made the argument,
I think, quite well

01:39:24.410 --> 01:39:27.890
that there is some evidence that
some progress has been made.

01:39:27.890 --> 01:39:29.390
There is some
evidence that suggests

01:39:29.390 --> 01:39:33.080
that since September 11, maybe
we've become more humane.

01:39:33.080 --> 01:39:35.390
Maybe we've become a
little less nativist.

01:39:35.390 --> 01:39:37.100
Maybe we've become
better neighbors.

01:39:37.100 --> 01:39:39.978
Maybe there is a little
less racial tension.

01:39:39.978 --> 01:39:40.520
I don't know.

01:39:40.520 --> 01:39:43.580
I'm not a scientist, and I've
not done a study on this.

01:39:43.580 --> 01:39:46.100
All I know is that James
Baldwin put it this way.

01:39:46.100 --> 01:39:49.250
Race is not a personal reality.

01:39:49.250 --> 01:39:51.590
Race is not a human reality.

01:39:51.590 --> 01:39:54.410
Race is a political reality.

01:39:54.410 --> 01:39:56.990
And for those folks who
are still black up in here,

01:39:56.990 --> 01:40:00.590
up in here, you understand
that Baldwin was right then,

01:40:00.590 --> 01:40:06.140
and he's right now that race
is still a political reality

01:40:06.140 --> 01:40:07.460
period.

01:40:07.460 --> 01:40:10.340
Now, it seems to
me that if we're

01:40:10.340 --> 01:40:13.520
going to raise this question
of this defining moment

01:40:13.520 --> 01:40:15.710
and how we redefine
America, then there's

01:40:15.710 --> 01:40:18.860
a role for each of us to play
in the process of answering

01:40:18.860 --> 01:40:20.923
that unique question.

01:40:20.923 --> 01:40:23.090
And while there's evidence,
as I said a moment ago--

01:40:23.090 --> 01:40:24.350
or intimated at least--

01:40:24.350 --> 01:40:25.850
while there's
evidence that suggests

01:40:25.850 --> 01:40:29.540
that we have made some
progress, I believe,

01:40:29.540 --> 01:40:33.720
I believe that there's
a great deal more to do.

01:40:33.720 --> 01:40:37.970
And for those who are
excited about the good that

01:40:37.970 --> 01:40:40.850
has come out of September
11, for those who believe

01:40:40.850 --> 01:40:43.790
this country is on a new path,
for those who believe that this

01:40:43.790 --> 01:40:46.580
is a new day, for those who
believe we've already gotten

01:40:46.580 --> 01:40:50.540
the point that America has
already been made exponentially

01:40:50.540 --> 01:40:54.270
better by September 11,
from my perspective--

01:40:54.270 --> 01:40:58.890
my humble perspective-- let me
disabuse you of that notion.

01:40:58.890 --> 01:41:02.990
I do not believe that
enough has been done.

01:41:02.990 --> 01:41:06.760
I don't believe, quite
frankly, that we get it yet.

01:41:06.760 --> 01:41:10.520
I do not believe that we get it.

01:41:10.520 --> 01:41:15.650
We still do not understand what
September 11 was all about.

01:41:15.650 --> 01:41:17.730
We don't get it.

01:41:17.730 --> 01:41:19.230
Every defining
moment-- I think it's

01:41:19.230 --> 01:41:21.750
important to note that every
defining moment does not

01:41:21.750 --> 01:41:25.410
necessarily lead to a
redefinition of anything.

01:41:25.410 --> 01:41:27.990
I know this is MIT, and I
know y'all believe in cause

01:41:27.990 --> 01:41:30.320
and effect.

01:41:30.320 --> 01:41:33.730
And we are told for every
cause, there is an effect.

01:41:33.730 --> 01:41:36.170
And I guess, you know, I
guess on some level that

01:41:36.170 --> 01:41:37.560
must be true.

01:41:37.560 --> 01:41:42.390
But it seems to me that we
have a cause for September 11,

01:41:42.390 --> 01:41:46.310
but I'm just not sure yet of
what the effect of September 11

01:41:46.310 --> 01:41:49.020
is yet or what it is
going to be long term.

01:41:49.020 --> 01:41:51.590
I am convinced of this,
that every defining

01:41:51.590 --> 01:41:54.110
moment in this country's
history does not necessarily

01:41:54.110 --> 01:41:56.120
redefine anything.

01:41:56.120 --> 01:41:57.745
Just because you have
a defining moment

01:41:57.745 --> 01:41:59.745
doesn't mean there's going
to be a redefinition.

01:41:59.745 --> 01:42:01.460
Can I convinced you
of this right quick?

01:42:01.460 --> 01:42:06.720
The Civil War was a
defining moment for America.

01:42:06.720 --> 01:42:08.910
On the radio show yesterday,
we had Morgan Freeman

01:42:08.910 --> 01:42:10.980
on the show, who
many of you recall

01:42:10.980 --> 01:42:13.710
was in that wonderful movie,
Glory, about the Massachusetts

01:42:13.710 --> 01:42:15.497
54th Colored Regiment.

01:42:15.497 --> 01:42:17.580
Denzel Washington, of
course, won an Academy Award

01:42:17.580 --> 01:42:19.357
for his wonderful
portrayal in that movie.

01:42:19.357 --> 01:42:20.940
But we had Morgan
on yesterday talking

01:42:20.940 --> 01:42:25.320
about this regiment in
the context of February

01:42:25.320 --> 01:42:28.560
and Black History Month, which
we now celebrate, of course.

01:42:28.560 --> 01:42:31.608
And it occurred to me in
our conversation yesterday--

01:42:31.608 --> 01:42:33.150
and I've written
about this of late--

01:42:33.150 --> 01:42:36.250
that while the Civil War
was a defining moment,

01:42:36.250 --> 01:42:40.113
what did it do to
redefine America?

01:42:40.113 --> 01:42:41.280
Specifically for black folk?

01:42:43.940 --> 01:42:46.740
This blows me away every
time I think about this.

01:42:46.740 --> 01:42:49.540
Understand that
in the Civil War--

01:42:49.540 --> 01:42:52.860
it is moving every
time I consider this.

01:42:52.860 --> 01:42:56.480
Here we are in the Civil
War with black folk

01:42:56.480 --> 01:43:03.016
who were, at best, indentured
servants, at worst,

01:43:03.016 --> 01:43:05.430
out and out slaves.

01:43:05.430 --> 01:43:07.230
At best, indentured servants.

01:43:07.230 --> 01:43:08.980
At worst, slaves.

01:43:08.980 --> 01:43:11.520
And yet these
Negroes volunteered

01:43:11.520 --> 01:43:16.260
to serve, and to fight, and
to die for their country.

01:43:18.980 --> 01:43:21.290
I don't think you
feel me on this yet.

01:43:21.290 --> 01:43:25.130
Black folk who were
slaves fighting,

01:43:25.130 --> 01:43:29.720
mind you, over the
institution of slavery,

01:43:29.720 --> 01:43:31.580
volunteered to
serve, and to fight,

01:43:31.580 --> 01:43:35.210
and to die for their country.

01:43:35.210 --> 01:43:38.000
That was a defining moment.

01:43:38.000 --> 01:43:41.274
But what did it do to redefine
America for black folk?

01:43:41.274 --> 01:43:43.610
Nothing.

01:43:43.610 --> 01:43:45.200
Fast forward.

01:43:45.200 --> 01:43:49.100
World War II, a defining
moment in this country.

01:43:49.100 --> 01:43:50.630
Negro soldiers,
colored soldiers,

01:43:50.630 --> 01:43:55.093
went to war to fight and to
die to serve their country.

01:43:55.093 --> 01:43:56.510
A defining moment,
but what did it

01:43:56.510 --> 01:43:59.150
do for black folk in America?

01:43:59.150 --> 01:44:00.380
Nothing.

01:44:00.380 --> 01:44:02.690
Do you realize that these
were colored soldiers,

01:44:02.690 --> 01:44:08.330
these were Negro soldiers,
on the train on the way home.

01:44:08.330 --> 01:44:09.330
Understand this.

01:44:09.330 --> 01:44:13.000
On the train after fighting
on the way back home,

01:44:13.000 --> 01:44:15.030
Negro soldiers,
colored soldiers,

01:44:15.030 --> 01:44:21.510
had to sit on the train
behind Nazi war criminals.

01:44:21.510 --> 01:44:23.640
Sitting on the train
behind Nazi war criminals.

01:44:23.640 --> 01:44:25.940
A defining moment.

01:44:25.940 --> 01:44:29.990
But what did it do to redefine
America for black folk?

01:44:29.990 --> 01:44:31.160
Nothing.

01:44:31.160 --> 01:44:34.250
The Vietnam War,
a defining moment.

01:44:34.250 --> 01:44:37.030
What did it do to redefine
America for black folk?

01:44:37.030 --> 01:44:38.118
Nothing.

01:44:38.118 --> 01:44:38.660
Muhammad Ali.

01:44:38.660 --> 01:44:39.410
You saw the movie.

01:44:39.410 --> 01:44:40.990
Muhammad Ali had this down.

01:44:40.990 --> 01:44:42.100
He understood it.

01:44:42.100 --> 01:44:44.470
And that's why we revere and
respect Ali so much today.

01:44:44.470 --> 01:44:46.780
Of the war in
Vietnam, Muhammad Ali

01:44:46.780 --> 01:44:49.420
put it down as only
he could say it.

01:44:49.420 --> 01:44:51.820
"You keep asking me,
no matter how long,

01:44:51.820 --> 01:44:53.160
about the war in Vietnam.

01:44:53.160 --> 01:44:54.760
I sing this song.

01:44:54.760 --> 01:44:57.970
I ain't got no quarrel
with the Viet Cong."

01:44:57.970 --> 01:44:59.650
Ali understood that.

01:44:59.650 --> 01:45:02.560
He understood that the Vietnam
War was a defining moment.

01:45:02.560 --> 01:45:06.270
But what was it going
to do to redefine

01:45:06.270 --> 01:45:09.300
America for black folk?

01:45:09.300 --> 01:45:13.170
Fast forward now to
America's new war.

01:45:13.170 --> 01:45:16.620
Here we are in 2002
in America's new war.

01:45:16.620 --> 01:45:18.900
And the question, that
urgent question, persists.

01:45:22.060 --> 01:45:25.150
What will happen in
this defining moment

01:45:25.150 --> 01:45:29.860
to redefine America
for black folk?

01:45:29.860 --> 01:45:31.880
Now, let me take this
concept a little further.

01:45:31.880 --> 01:45:34.370
The reason why I asked the
question about redefining

01:45:34.370 --> 01:45:37.340
America for black folk
is because I believe

01:45:37.340 --> 01:45:39.620
in the context of
Dr. King celebration,

01:45:39.620 --> 01:45:41.923
and the context of the
struggle, in the context

01:45:41.923 --> 01:45:43.340
of African American
History Month,

01:45:43.340 --> 01:45:45.970
you've got to start at
that particular place.

01:45:45.970 --> 01:45:47.300
You got to start with us.

01:45:47.300 --> 01:45:50.840
But I further believe that when
you make black America better,

01:45:50.840 --> 01:45:54.260
you make all of America better.

01:45:54.260 --> 01:45:57.410
You make black America better,
you make all of America better.

01:45:57.410 --> 01:45:59.330
So I want to be clear
about why I'm starting

01:45:59.330 --> 01:46:01.363
with that thesis at least.

01:46:01.363 --> 01:46:02.780
You make our
community better, you

01:46:02.780 --> 01:46:06.287
make the country a better place.

01:46:06.287 --> 01:46:07.870
But what is this
defining moment going

01:46:07.870 --> 01:46:10.270
to do to redefine
America for black folk

01:46:10.270 --> 01:46:12.807
and for all people of color?

01:46:12.807 --> 01:46:14.890
I hope I've just convinced
you that every defining

01:46:14.890 --> 01:46:17.140
moment doesn't
necessarily redefine,

01:46:17.140 --> 01:46:19.930
which means that somebody
has got to be prepared

01:46:19.930 --> 01:46:25.290
to do something, to take
advantage of a defining moment,

01:46:25.290 --> 01:46:28.080
and use it to redefine.

01:46:28.080 --> 01:46:29.010
How does one do that?

01:46:29.010 --> 01:46:31.800
I believe that one
does that by accepting

01:46:31.800 --> 01:46:37.230
the challenge that King gave us
to reexamine our assumptions.

01:46:37.230 --> 01:46:39.660
We have to reexamine
our assumptions

01:46:39.660 --> 01:46:41.600
about a variety of things.

01:46:41.600 --> 01:46:43.350
The president mentioned
in my introduction

01:46:43.350 --> 01:46:45.090
that there are a
number of media outlets

01:46:45.090 --> 01:46:47.190
that we are now employed by.

01:46:47.190 --> 01:46:49.930
You know, BET was great,
and it was wonderful run,

01:46:49.930 --> 01:46:51.360
but thank God that life goes on.

01:46:51.360 --> 01:46:53.490
And now there's ABC,
and there's CNN.

01:46:53.490 --> 01:46:55.670
There's NPR and all the
other wonderful things

01:46:55.670 --> 01:46:58.140
that we get a chance
to do on a daily basis

01:46:58.140 --> 01:46:59.600
vis-a-vis the media.

01:46:59.600 --> 01:47:01.380
A quick story.

01:47:01.380 --> 01:47:04.398
Months ago, I signed
this contract with CNN

01:47:04.398 --> 01:47:05.940
and started doing
commentary for them

01:47:05.940 --> 01:47:08.380
during the day side
and prime time for ABC

01:47:08.380 --> 01:47:10.260
on Thursday evenings.

01:47:10.260 --> 01:47:13.033
And I signed the contract,
and I appeared on CNN

01:47:13.033 --> 01:47:13.950
for a couple of weeks.

01:47:13.950 --> 01:47:16.500
And then, bam, September 11
came, and all of a sudden,

01:47:16.500 --> 01:47:17.682
I kind of disappeared.

01:47:17.682 --> 01:47:19.890
So black folks all across
the country, indeed others,

01:47:19.890 --> 01:47:22.920
but certainly black folk were
writing CNN, and e-mailing me,

01:47:22.920 --> 01:47:26.932
and calling me, and faxing
me, and asking me, Travis--

01:47:26.932 --> 01:47:30.860
[LAUGHTER]

01:47:30.860 --> 01:47:34.420
That's a joke for those
who really know my name.

01:47:34.420 --> 01:47:37.070
They were saying to me,
Travis, we saw you on CNN

01:47:37.070 --> 01:47:38.010
a couple of weeks ago.

01:47:38.010 --> 01:47:41.130
And now that the war has hit,
we want your perspective.

01:47:41.130 --> 01:47:43.594
Where you at?

01:47:43.594 --> 01:47:45.140
And as my English
teacher would tell,

01:47:45.140 --> 01:47:47.950
you know, behind
that preposition--

01:47:47.950 --> 01:47:50.060
but they'd ask me, where you at?

01:47:50.060 --> 01:47:51.920
We haven't seen you lately.

01:47:51.920 --> 01:47:54.140
And I had to go on
the radio one day,

01:47:54.140 --> 01:47:57.110
after weeks of getting all this
mail and all these inquiries,

01:47:57.110 --> 01:47:58.800
because I needed to
address this issue.

01:47:58.800 --> 01:48:01.310
I didn't want folk to think
that I'd been hired by CNN

01:48:01.310 --> 01:48:03.718
and fired that
quickly, you know?

01:48:03.718 --> 01:48:04.760
So I had to explain this.

01:48:04.760 --> 01:48:07.218
So I went on the radio said to
people, let me just tell you

01:48:07.218 --> 01:48:11.360
where I've been at and why you
haven't seen me on CNN or ABC

01:48:11.360 --> 01:48:14.780
talking about the war, talking
about the attack of September

01:48:14.780 --> 01:48:15.430
11.

01:48:15.430 --> 01:48:18.110
This is just a few weeks
removed from the 11th.

01:48:18.110 --> 01:48:20.180
I said to them very
quickly and essentially

01:48:20.180 --> 01:48:22.310
that I was watching
Politically Incorrect one night

01:48:22.310 --> 01:48:23.300
with my friend Bill Maher.

01:48:23.300 --> 01:48:24.508
Bill's a dear friend of mine.

01:48:24.508 --> 01:48:25.400
I love the show.

01:48:25.400 --> 01:48:27.230
But I'm watching
Bill's show one night,

01:48:27.230 --> 01:48:29.343
and I see Bill put
his foot in his mouth.

01:48:29.343 --> 01:48:31.760
And some of y'all have followed
this controversy with Bill

01:48:31.760 --> 01:48:33.052
Maher on Politically Incorrect.

01:48:33.052 --> 01:48:35.180
And there is still
a very likelihood

01:48:35.180 --> 01:48:36.910
that he will lose his
show, that the show

01:48:36.910 --> 01:48:38.660
will be canceled at
the end of the season,

01:48:38.660 --> 01:48:42.280
because Bill said the wrong
thing at the wrong time.

01:48:42.280 --> 01:48:44.750
Some of you recall that
Bill Maher suggested

01:48:44.750 --> 01:48:47.150
in a politically incorrect
format, which that's

01:48:47.150 --> 01:48:49.870
what his show is, so I don't
know why he got in trouble,

01:48:49.870 --> 01:48:51.650
but I digress.

01:48:51.650 --> 01:48:56.067
Bill said in the show that
they had a lot of courage.

01:48:56.067 --> 01:48:57.900
It took a lot of courage
for these terrorist

01:48:57.900 --> 01:48:59.755
to train, and to
do what they did,

01:48:59.755 --> 01:49:00.880
and to dedicate themselves.

01:49:00.880 --> 01:49:02.900
It was wicked courage,
but courage to come

01:49:02.900 --> 01:49:05.390
do what they did and to
plan it the way they did.

01:49:05.390 --> 01:49:08.530
And Bill suggested that
we are a bunch of cowards,

01:49:08.530 --> 01:49:10.607
that we were over here
lobbing cruise missiles.

01:49:10.607 --> 01:49:12.440
We don't have the courage
of our convictions

01:49:12.440 --> 01:49:13.940
to do what they did.

01:49:13.940 --> 01:49:16.250
And I understood his analogy.

01:49:16.250 --> 01:49:19.610
But the Bible puts it this
way, all things are lawful,

01:49:19.610 --> 01:49:22.670
but all things
are not expedient.

01:49:22.670 --> 01:49:23.170
All right.

01:49:23.170 --> 01:49:26.140
Some of y'all didn't get that.

01:49:26.140 --> 01:49:27.580
Let me come at that another way.

01:49:27.580 --> 01:49:30.520
How about how
about Kenny Rogers?

01:49:30.520 --> 01:49:34.790
You got to know when to hold
'em and know when to fold 'em.

01:49:34.790 --> 01:49:35.290
All right.

01:49:35.290 --> 01:49:37.950
Now I got you.

01:49:37.950 --> 01:49:40.370
Know when to walk away,
and know when to run.

01:49:40.370 --> 01:49:43.900
You know, Malcolm X
had his relationship

01:49:43.900 --> 01:49:45.680
with Elijah Muhammad
start to deteriorate.

01:49:45.680 --> 01:49:46.375
When?

01:49:46.375 --> 01:49:50.020
When after the assassination
of JFK, Malcolm X said,

01:49:50.020 --> 01:49:53.870
the chickens have
come home to roost.

01:49:53.870 --> 01:49:57.627
Wrong thing at the wrong time.

01:49:57.627 --> 01:49:59.210
So Bill Maher found
himself in trouble

01:49:59.210 --> 01:50:01.085
for saying the wrong
thing at the wrong time.

01:50:01.085 --> 01:50:02.870
I saw Bill get in
trouble with that.

01:50:02.870 --> 01:50:03.620
I said, OK.

01:50:03.620 --> 01:50:04.537
I'm just making notes.

01:50:04.537 --> 01:50:07.342
I'm paying attention.

01:50:07.342 --> 01:50:08.800
I'm listening to
the radio one day,

01:50:08.800 --> 01:50:11.450
and I hear Rush Limbaugh
on the radio one day

01:50:11.450 --> 01:50:17.470
go into overdrive blaming the
September 11 attack on William

01:50:17.470 --> 01:50:19.600
Jefferson Clinton.

01:50:19.600 --> 01:50:20.670
Go figure.

01:50:20.670 --> 01:50:24.390
But Limbaugh finds a way to
blame the attack on September

01:50:24.390 --> 01:50:28.310
11 on the failed policies of
the Clinton administration.

01:50:28.310 --> 01:50:30.060
That's why I love my
friends on the right.

01:50:30.060 --> 01:50:34.020
They will always find a way to
blame Clinton for something.

01:50:34.020 --> 01:50:36.180
And so Rush got in
trouble for really

01:50:36.180 --> 01:50:39.300
jumping out too
headstrong politically

01:50:39.300 --> 01:50:40.680
blaming this on Clinton.

01:50:40.680 --> 01:50:42.630
Just inappropriate.

01:50:42.630 --> 01:50:44.730
Limbaugh got slapped
down, and got in trouble,

01:50:44.730 --> 01:50:47.613
and was being challenged in
the media for what he had said.

01:50:47.613 --> 01:50:49.530
And then I looked up one
day and saw my friend

01:50:49.530 --> 01:50:52.800
the right Reverend Dr. Jerry
Falwell put both of his feet,

01:50:52.800 --> 01:50:57.390
hands, everything
else in his mouth

01:50:57.390 --> 01:51:00.300
when Falwell suggested that
the attacks could be blamed

01:51:00.300 --> 01:51:06.270
on the civil libertarians, on
the feminists, on the pagans,

01:51:06.270 --> 01:51:08.308
on black folk, on the ACLU.

01:51:08.308 --> 01:51:09.600
Some of y'all saw this comment.

01:51:09.600 --> 01:51:11.995
You know I'm not making this up.

01:51:11.995 --> 01:51:12.870
Did anybody see this?

01:51:12.870 --> 01:51:14.370
Makes sure-- OK.

01:51:14.370 --> 01:51:15.720
I want to make sure I'm not
talking to myself up here.

01:51:15.720 --> 01:51:16.710
I'm not making this up.

01:51:16.710 --> 01:51:17.585
This is a true story.

01:51:17.585 --> 01:51:19.650
So Jerry Falwell got in
trouble for blaming it

01:51:19.650 --> 01:51:24.120
on basically everybody
except the Christian right.

01:51:24.120 --> 01:51:26.610
I said to the audience, after
recounting these stories,

01:51:26.610 --> 01:51:33.430
that by my count, that's one,
two, three white boys who all

01:51:33.430 --> 01:51:36.010
got in trouble for
running they mouth,

01:51:36.010 --> 01:51:39.240
saying the wrong thing
at the wrong time.

01:51:39.240 --> 01:51:45.890
I know if I take my fresh
black behind up on CNN and ABC

01:51:45.890 --> 01:51:49.460
and put it down the way I
know I want to put it down,

01:51:49.460 --> 01:51:51.140
I would be fired
with the quickness--

01:51:51.140 --> 01:51:56.570
and you would never ever hear
the name Travis Smiley again.

01:51:56.570 --> 01:51:58.175
And so it wasn't the time.

01:52:01.790 --> 01:52:06.250
The point is that the
time just wasn't right

01:52:06.250 --> 01:52:09.220
for me to say what I
thought needed to be said.

01:52:09.220 --> 01:52:11.770
The time wasn't
necessarily right to ask

01:52:11.770 --> 01:52:13.025
these tough questions.

01:52:13.025 --> 01:52:15.150
But oh, brothers and sisters,
ladies and gentlemen,

01:52:15.150 --> 01:52:17.800
there is a time a coming.

01:52:17.800 --> 01:52:20.350
And maybe that time is now.

01:52:20.350 --> 01:52:24.730
And maybe today is the moment
that those of us who are

01:52:24.730 --> 01:52:31.000
dedicated to the principle of
making America better after

01:52:31.000 --> 01:52:34.830
September 11, of making this
country live up to her true

01:52:34.830 --> 01:52:38.100
ideals, of making these
documents-- the Constitution,

01:52:38.100 --> 01:52:40.350
and the Declaration of
Independence in this great city

01:52:40.350 --> 01:52:42.960
of Boston--

01:52:42.960 --> 01:52:46.620
making our history real,
making these documents living

01:52:46.620 --> 01:52:47.880
and breathing documents.

01:52:47.880 --> 01:52:50.730
For those who are dedicated
to the proposition

01:52:50.730 --> 01:52:54.090
that America can be
made better, there

01:52:54.090 --> 01:53:00.030
is no better time than now for
us to reexamine our assumptions

01:53:00.030 --> 01:53:06.280
and to take this defining
moment and redefine America.

01:53:06.280 --> 01:53:09.150
We got to ask some tough
questions to do that.

01:53:09.150 --> 01:53:11.920
Can I offer a few of them
to you as we leave here?

01:53:11.920 --> 01:53:16.020
I believe we got to start asking
the question of what patriotism

01:53:16.020 --> 01:53:17.760
really is.

01:53:17.760 --> 01:53:20.100
Let me get myself up
some trouble right quick.

01:53:20.100 --> 01:53:21.190
We got the check already?

01:53:21.190 --> 01:53:21.690
All right.

01:53:21.690 --> 01:53:25.440
[LAUGHTER]

01:53:25.440 --> 01:53:28.050
You know, some places, you got
to ask, do you have to check,

01:53:28.050 --> 01:53:28.920
Mr. Osgood.

01:53:28.920 --> 01:53:33.210
Some places, you've got to
ask, have we cashed the check.

01:53:33.210 --> 01:53:35.460
I know at MIT I ain't got
to worry about that problem.

01:53:35.460 --> 01:53:35.960
All right.

01:53:38.730 --> 01:53:42.060
I believe we have to
start by asking ourselves

01:53:42.060 --> 01:53:46.203
what patriotism really
is and what it means.

01:53:46.203 --> 01:53:48.120
Can I just be real with
you for just a second?

01:53:48.120 --> 01:53:49.920
Can I keep it real
for just a minute?

01:53:49.920 --> 01:53:52.190
I've noticed a distinction here.

01:53:52.190 --> 01:53:53.490
There are two things going on.

01:53:53.490 --> 01:53:56.420
Some of us are caught
up in the patriotism,

01:53:56.420 --> 01:53:58.490
and I ain't mad at you.

01:53:58.490 --> 01:54:02.020
Some of us, though, have gotten
caught up in nationalism.

01:54:02.020 --> 01:54:03.850
There is a distinction.

01:54:03.850 --> 01:54:05.680
Patriotism is one thing.

01:54:05.680 --> 01:54:09.820
Patriotism demands that you
make your country better

01:54:09.820 --> 01:54:11.770
by asking the tough questions.

01:54:11.770 --> 01:54:14.110
Patriotism demands debate.

01:54:14.110 --> 01:54:16.750
Patriotism sometimes
demands dissent.

01:54:16.750 --> 01:54:20.160
Nationalism is fanaticism.

01:54:20.160 --> 01:54:20.910
I'm an American.

01:54:20.910 --> 01:54:21.952
I'm going to wave a flag.

01:54:21.952 --> 01:54:24.930
I'm down with whatever
Mr. Bush says.

01:54:24.930 --> 01:54:27.240
Whatever Congress
says, I'm down with it.

01:54:27.240 --> 01:54:30.150
And nobody's prepared
to stand in the gap

01:54:30.150 --> 01:54:34.180
and to raise the tough questions
that need to be raised.

01:54:34.180 --> 01:54:35.430
And that is not an indictment.

01:54:35.430 --> 01:54:37.950
I mean to cast no aspersion
necessarily on the way

01:54:37.950 --> 01:54:41.240
that Mr. Bush has stewarded
this country since September 11.

01:54:41.240 --> 01:54:43.560
I think, on balance,
he's done a fine job.

01:54:43.560 --> 01:54:47.610
But I believe that somewhere
in this debate about patriotism

01:54:47.610 --> 01:54:51.820
were King here with
us today, he would

01:54:51.820 --> 01:54:56.530
be demanding that we make the
distinction between patriotism

01:54:56.530 --> 01:54:58.590
and nationalism.

01:54:58.590 --> 01:55:01.940
He would demand that we raise
the tough questions about what

01:55:01.940 --> 01:55:05.280
it really means
to be an American.

01:55:05.280 --> 01:55:08.060
He would demand that we raise
some tough questions about how

01:55:08.060 --> 01:55:11.075
we can become a better
neighbor in the world.

01:55:11.075 --> 01:55:12.950
It's not enough to stand
at this podium today

01:55:12.950 --> 01:55:15.820
and to call off the names of
all these wonderful countries

01:55:15.820 --> 01:55:18.320
that we have Nobel
laureates from

01:55:18.320 --> 01:55:20.210
and that we have students from.

01:55:20.210 --> 01:55:22.220
MIT is a great institution.

01:55:22.220 --> 01:55:26.150
But the truth of the
matter is that, in part, we

01:55:26.150 --> 01:55:30.080
found ourselves in this mess on
September 11 because of the way

01:55:30.080 --> 01:55:32.875
we treat folk around the world.

01:55:32.875 --> 01:55:34.000
We don't want to deal with.

01:55:34.000 --> 01:55:35.880
We don't want to deal with that.

01:55:35.880 --> 01:55:39.640
[APPLAUSE]

01:55:39.640 --> 01:55:42.270
You've got to ask
some tough questions.

01:55:42.270 --> 01:55:44.850
The chickens have
come home to roost.

01:55:44.850 --> 01:55:49.050
You can't prop up dictators
like Mobutu Sese Seko in parts

01:55:49.050 --> 01:55:52.250
of Africa, befriending
anybody who would befriend you

01:55:52.250 --> 01:55:54.410
during the Cold
War, and not think

01:55:54.410 --> 01:55:56.720
that will come back to
haunt you at some point.

01:55:56.720 --> 01:55:59.750
You can't prop up dictators
like Marcos in the Philippines

01:55:59.750 --> 01:56:03.140
and think that won't come home
to haunt you at some time.

01:56:03.140 --> 01:56:05.870
And the list goes on
and on and on and on

01:56:05.870 --> 01:56:08.030
of, quite frankly,
transgressions

01:56:08.030 --> 01:56:12.490
that we have made,
mistakes that we have made.

01:56:12.490 --> 01:56:17.020
And I hope, at some point,
that somebody has the courage,

01:56:17.020 --> 01:56:19.450
that somebody has the
fortitude, that somebody

01:56:19.450 --> 01:56:22.180
has the good sense to raise
the kinds of questions

01:56:22.180 --> 01:56:25.000
about the very
notion of patriotism

01:56:25.000 --> 01:56:27.640
and how we behave or
misbehave in the world

01:56:27.640 --> 01:56:30.160
as Americans trying
to big stick people.

01:56:30.160 --> 01:56:32.410
Somebody has got to
raise these questions.

01:56:32.410 --> 01:56:34.090
And if King were here today--

01:56:34.090 --> 01:56:36.590
you're right-- he wouldn't let
you get away with calling him

01:56:36.590 --> 01:56:38.140
just a dreamer.

01:56:38.140 --> 01:56:41.080
He would challenge us to ask
the tough questions about what

01:56:41.080 --> 01:56:46.220
it means to be an American, how
we can become better Americans,

01:56:46.220 --> 01:56:49.650
and what patriotism
really means.

01:56:49.650 --> 01:56:51.330
King would demand,
were he had today,

01:56:51.330 --> 01:56:54.820
that we ask some questions
about foreign policy.

01:56:54.820 --> 01:56:59.160
Have you noticed that when
Bush took office, Bush,

01:56:59.160 --> 01:57:06.470
Rumsfeld, Cheney, and Rice were
all lined up as unilateralists.

01:57:06.470 --> 01:57:07.220
It was very clear.

01:57:07.220 --> 01:57:09.650
It's our way or the highway.

01:57:09.650 --> 01:57:10.760
It was hawkish.

01:57:10.760 --> 01:57:12.950
It was John Wayne.

01:57:12.950 --> 01:57:14.690
It was to hell with
the Kosovo treaty.

01:57:14.690 --> 01:57:15.830
We ain't signing it.

01:57:15.830 --> 01:57:17.130
We going to get out of it.

01:57:17.130 --> 01:57:20.070
We're not going to South
Africa to no racism conference.

01:57:20.070 --> 01:57:23.290
No, we ain't going.

01:57:23.290 --> 01:57:27.580
Our way or the highway, that
was the unilateral approach

01:57:27.580 --> 01:57:29.110
when they came into office.

01:57:29.110 --> 01:57:32.110
Oh my, how things have changed.

01:57:32.110 --> 01:57:34.930
Colin Powell was the only
one standing over here

01:57:34.930 --> 01:57:37.380
with a multilateral
approach, saying that we

01:57:37.380 --> 01:57:39.640
have got to engage the world.

01:57:39.640 --> 01:57:41.170
We've got to be
better neighbors.

01:57:41.170 --> 01:57:44.570
We got to rethink how we
do our policy, et cetera,

01:57:44.570 --> 01:57:45.730
et cetera, et cetera.

01:57:45.730 --> 01:57:47.270
They didn't want to hear that.

01:57:47.270 --> 01:57:52.170
But now, I have noted at least
of late the administration now

01:57:52.170 --> 01:57:54.500
speaking more with one voice.

01:57:54.500 --> 01:57:56.520
And maybe now, they're
starting to get the fact

01:57:56.520 --> 01:58:01.020
that we have to have a
multilateral, a we, not

01:58:01.020 --> 01:58:05.810
an us versus them approach,
to how we engage the world.

01:58:05.810 --> 01:58:09.860
But why did it have to come
at the expense of those 3,000

01:58:09.860 --> 01:58:12.230
lives at the World Trade
Center and the Pentagon

01:58:12.230 --> 01:58:13.333
and in those planes?

01:58:13.333 --> 01:58:14.750
We ought to have
better sense then

01:58:14.750 --> 01:58:17.990
to understand that we've
got to have a better way

01:58:17.990 --> 01:58:22.140
to engage our neighbors and
our friends around the globe.

01:58:22.140 --> 01:58:25.190
We've got to raise some tough
questions about foreign policy.

01:58:25.190 --> 01:58:28.040
Everybody gets caught up in
this euphoria about Dr. King,

01:58:28.040 --> 01:58:33.230
and nobody recalls that King
had dropped precipitously

01:58:33.230 --> 01:58:36.530
in opinion polls at
the time of his death.

01:58:36.530 --> 01:58:38.420
Go back and check your facts.

01:58:38.420 --> 01:58:41.360
King was not at the top
of the popularity polls

01:58:41.360 --> 01:58:45.110
when he died because
of his opposition

01:58:45.110 --> 01:58:46.475
to the war in Vietnam.

01:58:49.205 --> 01:58:51.250
As I recall-- don't
quote me on this--

01:58:51.250 --> 01:58:53.590
I don't even think
King made the top five.

01:58:53.590 --> 01:58:56.680
He may have barely made
the top 10 in the Time poll

01:58:56.680 --> 01:59:00.220
at the time of his death based
upon popularity and likability.

01:59:00.220 --> 01:59:04.910
That q-rating that he had
had dropped dramatically.

01:59:04.910 --> 01:59:06.470
King understood that.

01:59:06.470 --> 01:59:08.435
He wasn't bothered by it.

01:59:08.435 --> 01:59:10.310
He wasn't bothered by
because King understood

01:59:10.310 --> 01:59:12.560
that the ultimate
measure of a man

01:59:12.560 --> 01:59:15.930
is not where he stands in times
of comfort and convenience,

01:59:15.930 --> 01:59:20.450
but where he stands in times
of controversy and challenge.

01:59:20.450 --> 01:59:24.240
Somebody's got to ask the tough
questions about foreign policy.

01:59:24.240 --> 01:59:27.110
It's not just a celebration
of the life and legacy of Dr.

01:59:27.110 --> 01:59:28.730
King, but understanding
that we have

01:59:28.730 --> 01:59:33.110
to reexamine the assumptions.

01:59:33.110 --> 01:59:37.590
This issue of civil liberties
that are, quite frankly, out

01:59:37.590 --> 01:59:38.090
of control.

01:59:40.980 --> 01:59:43.745
I don't feel safer
today with regard

01:59:43.745 --> 01:59:45.370
to the issue of racial
profiling than I

01:59:45.370 --> 01:59:49.720
did a few days ago just
because somebody else is now

01:59:49.720 --> 01:59:51.310
the target.

01:59:51.310 --> 01:59:52.210
No, no, no, no, no.

01:59:52.210 --> 01:59:56.930
I know too many brothers in my
neighborhood in South Central

01:59:56.930 --> 01:59:59.780
who look like some
Arabs that we think

01:59:59.780 --> 02:00:01.490
are responsible
for what happened

02:00:01.490 --> 02:00:02.750
who we want to go after.

02:00:02.750 --> 02:00:06.020
Are you feeling me on this?

02:00:06.020 --> 02:00:09.663
I know some folk of Arab
descent who look just like some

02:00:09.663 --> 02:00:11.210
of y'all up in this room today.

02:00:11.210 --> 02:00:13.650
The shade of their skin--

02:00:13.650 --> 02:00:15.020
are you feeling me on this?

02:00:15.020 --> 02:00:19.400
The shade of their skin is very
similar and very akin to yours.

02:00:19.400 --> 02:00:21.530
The problem is we have
an administration now

02:00:21.530 --> 02:00:26.525
that's doing an overreach
on civil liberties.

02:00:26.525 --> 02:00:27.900
I think we all
agree that there's

02:00:27.900 --> 02:00:29.940
got to be a balance
between civil liberties

02:00:29.940 --> 02:00:33.270
and doing away and
ending terrorism.

02:00:33.270 --> 02:00:36.300
But it's got to be done in
a culturally competent way.

02:00:36.300 --> 02:00:39.877
It's got to be done in a
socially responsible way.

02:00:39.877 --> 02:00:41.710
And I don't know that
we are achieving that.

02:00:41.710 --> 02:00:43.720
If King were here
today, he'd be asking

02:00:43.720 --> 02:00:47.770
us to reexamine our
assumptions about how we're

02:00:47.770 --> 02:00:52.360
going to go after
terrorists, about how

02:00:52.360 --> 02:00:54.940
we're going to condone this
racial profiling that's

02:00:54.940 --> 02:00:56.020
going on now.

02:00:56.020 --> 02:00:58.300
Once they get these laws,
once they get these rules

02:00:58.300 --> 02:01:01.597
on the books, as they're
getting every day, a few years

02:01:01.597 --> 02:01:03.180
from now, who do you
think these rules

02:01:03.180 --> 02:01:04.900
are going to be used against?

02:01:04.900 --> 02:01:08.230
You, driving while black,
walking while black,

02:01:08.230 --> 02:01:10.530
breathing while black,
living while black,

02:01:10.530 --> 02:01:14.950
just being black and brown.

02:01:14.950 --> 02:01:17.620
We got to reexamine
our assumptions

02:01:17.620 --> 02:01:24.116
about what it means to have this
debate about racial profiling.

02:01:24.116 --> 02:01:26.690
Got to reexamine our
assumptions about this.

02:01:26.690 --> 02:01:29.600
Somebody's got to ask some
tough questions about the media.

02:01:29.600 --> 02:01:32.540
I'm just giving you some food
for thought here this morning.

02:01:32.540 --> 02:01:36.330
Did you notice, during
all the coverage, here

02:01:36.330 --> 02:01:38.910
you have a city like
New York, arguably one

02:01:38.910 --> 02:01:43.160
of greatest cities in the
world, the most multicultural,

02:01:43.160 --> 02:01:48.420
multiracial, and multi-ethnic
city in the world.

02:01:48.420 --> 02:01:50.310
And yet, how many
people of color

02:01:50.310 --> 02:01:52.860
did you see reporting
the news to you

02:01:52.860 --> 02:01:54.910
every night out of New York?

02:01:54.910 --> 02:01:57.170
Some of y'all didn't
even peep that out.

02:02:00.970 --> 02:02:03.580
We're talking about coming
together as a nation.

02:02:03.580 --> 02:02:05.960
Do you see the irony here?

02:02:05.960 --> 02:02:09.053
Do you see, quite frankly,
the hypocrisy here?

02:02:09.053 --> 02:02:11.470
We are talking together about
coming together as a nation,

02:02:11.470 --> 02:02:13.162
and we shall overcome,
and God bless

02:02:13.162 --> 02:02:14.620
America, and America
the Beautiful,

02:02:14.620 --> 02:02:17.320
and yada, yada,
yada, yada, yada.

02:02:17.320 --> 02:02:19.600
And yet, I'm watching
the news every night.

02:02:19.600 --> 02:02:24.250
I don't see nobody looking
like me reporting the news.

02:02:24.250 --> 02:02:26.400
I don't see folk like
me, quite frankly,

02:02:26.400 --> 02:02:30.690
routinely who are
victims of this tragedy.

02:02:30.690 --> 02:02:33.690
I didn't see no cops
who looked like me.

02:02:33.690 --> 02:02:35.400
Wasn't no firefighters
who look like

02:02:35.400 --> 02:02:38.130
me because there ain't no black
firefighters in the New York

02:02:38.130 --> 02:02:40.830
Fire Department
comparatively speaking.

02:02:40.830 --> 02:02:43.770
My point is simply this--

02:02:43.770 --> 02:02:47.190
that while we talk
about coming together

02:02:47.190 --> 02:02:49.990
and the good that's come
out of this tragedy,

02:02:49.990 --> 02:02:55.760
there are some assumptions that
still need to be reexamined.

02:02:55.760 --> 02:02:59.185
And King would not let us
off the hook so easily.

02:02:59.185 --> 02:03:00.810
He wouldn't let us
off the hook talking

02:03:00.810 --> 02:03:04.770
about his life and his legacy
without understanding what

02:03:04.770 --> 02:03:07.800
his life and legacy was
about, getting us to reexamine

02:03:07.800 --> 02:03:10.650
the assumptions and to
challenge the prevailing wisdom

02:03:10.650 --> 02:03:14.610
to challenge the status quo.

02:03:14.610 --> 02:03:18.190
Nobody wants to have these
conversations right now,

02:03:18.190 --> 02:03:19.950
but these are the real issues.

02:03:19.950 --> 02:03:21.750
This is what really
has to happen

02:03:21.750 --> 02:03:26.920
if America is going to be made
better out of this tragedy.

02:03:26.920 --> 02:03:28.810
Somebody has got to
ask some questions

02:03:28.810 --> 02:03:34.240
about religious intolerance,
racial intolerance.

02:03:34.240 --> 02:03:37.172
You realize that hate is alive
and well in this country.

02:03:37.172 --> 02:03:39.380
They're not walking up and
down the street with hoods

02:03:39.380 --> 02:03:42.180
and sheets like they
used to, but hate

02:03:42.180 --> 02:03:44.490
is spreading in this country.

02:03:44.490 --> 02:03:47.040
And do you know where
hate is spreading fastest?

02:03:47.040 --> 02:03:49.240
On the internet.

02:03:49.240 --> 02:03:52.360
There are more sites popping
up every month dedicated

02:03:52.360 --> 02:03:55.600
to the notion of spreading
hate than any other genre

02:03:55.600 --> 02:03:58.393
on the net, any other
genre on the web.

02:03:58.393 --> 02:04:00.560
That's one reason, Mr.
President, one of the reasons

02:04:00.560 --> 02:04:02.207
why we're doing this
technology summit

02:04:02.207 --> 02:04:04.040
and partnering with
Bill Gates and traveling

02:04:04.040 --> 02:04:04.832
around the country.

02:04:04.832 --> 02:04:06.740
Gates and I were
together in LA last week,

02:04:06.740 --> 02:04:08.420
going to three other
cities this year

02:04:08.420 --> 02:04:10.640
with a whole tour called
Blacks and Technology.

02:04:10.640 --> 02:04:11.330
Free.

02:04:11.330 --> 02:04:12.180
Open to the public.

02:04:12.180 --> 02:04:14.472
Trying to get black folks to
understand, to appreciate,

02:04:14.472 --> 02:04:17.300
to embrace this thing
called technology.

02:04:17.300 --> 02:04:20.540
Because I am
determined to not allow

02:04:20.540 --> 02:04:23.682
this technological revolution
to pass people of color by

02:04:23.682 --> 02:04:25.640
and we not get a little
something something out

02:04:25.640 --> 02:04:26.870
of it, number one.

02:04:26.870 --> 02:04:28.580
But number two, to
allow this technology,

02:04:28.580 --> 02:04:30.740
as the president said, to
be turned in a wicked way

02:04:30.740 --> 02:04:32.930
and used against us.

02:04:32.930 --> 02:04:35.490
We've got to use it for
our good, for our benefit,

02:04:35.490 --> 02:04:38.890
and not allow other folks
to use it for our detriment.

02:04:38.890 --> 02:04:41.802
But hate is spreading
fastest on the internet.

02:04:41.802 --> 02:04:45.840
Nobody's talking about
technology and how we are

02:04:45.840 --> 02:04:49.360
being left out of that process.

02:04:49.360 --> 02:04:51.900
We talk about the technology
we have to go hunt people down

02:04:51.900 --> 02:04:52.692
and smoke them out.

02:04:52.692 --> 02:04:56.040
We're talking about technology
in terms of how we zoom in.

02:04:56.040 --> 02:04:58.770
And we're taking credit
for how we use technology

02:04:58.770 --> 02:05:01.560
to wipe out this building
and wipe out this truck.

02:05:01.560 --> 02:05:04.228
I mean, I'm fascinated
sometimes by how much we've

02:05:04.228 --> 02:05:05.520
done in our Defense Department.

02:05:05.520 --> 02:05:09.930
We can target a truck going
down the road 10,000 miles away

02:05:09.930 --> 02:05:14.460
and take out a truck on a
road with somebody in it

02:05:14.460 --> 02:05:16.260
who we want.

02:05:16.260 --> 02:05:17.790
And yet, we can't
get our schools

02:05:17.790 --> 02:05:19.117
wired in the inner city.

02:05:19.117 --> 02:05:19.617
Hello.

02:05:22.830 --> 02:05:26.470
Can't wire schools
in the inner city.

02:05:26.470 --> 02:05:29.930
But we can't have
that conversation.

02:05:29.930 --> 02:05:34.800
See we up here in the clouds
with the life and the legacy.

02:05:34.800 --> 02:05:36.730
And that ain't where King was.

02:05:36.730 --> 02:05:39.770
King was down here getting us
to reexamine the assumptions.

02:05:39.770 --> 02:05:45.660
Finally, there's a litany
of domestic policy issues

02:05:45.660 --> 02:05:48.870
that King would be
demanding that we talk about

02:05:48.870 --> 02:05:51.880
at the appropriate time.

02:05:51.880 --> 02:05:54.490
If we really are talking
about making America better,

02:05:54.490 --> 02:05:55.900
it can't just be MIT.

02:05:55.900 --> 02:05:58.720
It can't just be MIT
dedicated to the notion

02:05:58.720 --> 02:06:00.020
of affirmative action.

02:06:00.020 --> 02:06:02.747
We have to have a country
and a government that

02:06:02.747 --> 02:06:04.330
understands that
affirmative action is

02:06:04.330 --> 02:06:07.075
a corrective policy that works.

02:06:07.075 --> 02:06:08.450
Everybody has to
understand that.

02:06:08.450 --> 02:06:09.680
Got to understand that.

02:06:09.680 --> 02:06:11.950
[APPLAUSE]

02:06:11.950 --> 02:06:15.270
That's what King would
be talking about.

02:06:15.270 --> 02:06:19.660
We've got to do something
about predatory lending,

02:06:19.660 --> 02:06:21.700
about why we go
after people of color

02:06:21.700 --> 02:06:26.280
and the indigenous community
and predatory lending ways.

02:06:26.280 --> 02:06:27.633
One, you can't get no money.

02:06:27.633 --> 02:06:29.050
And if you get it,
they charge you

02:06:29.050 --> 02:06:31.300
more to get it because
of what you look like

02:06:31.300 --> 02:06:32.980
and where you live.

02:06:32.980 --> 02:06:35.930
Insurance redlining.

02:06:35.930 --> 02:06:38.710
I could do an end
run on you here.

02:06:38.710 --> 02:06:44.150
A long litany of things that
we are not talking about,

02:06:44.150 --> 02:06:46.730
that we are not debating,
because we have been stifled

02:06:46.730 --> 02:06:51.230
by this war on terrorism.

02:06:51.230 --> 02:06:53.740
Immigration policy.

02:06:53.740 --> 02:06:56.300
Education.

02:06:56.300 --> 02:06:58.742
A long list of things
that we have to address.

02:06:58.742 --> 02:07:00.450
And if King were here
today, he would not

02:07:00.450 --> 02:07:04.230
be leading us off the hook with
the mere notion of celebrating

02:07:04.230 --> 02:07:06.340
his life and his legacy
his wonderful work

02:07:06.340 --> 02:07:07.518
and his wonderful prose.

02:07:07.518 --> 02:07:09.060
He would be demanding
and challenging

02:07:09.060 --> 02:07:15.160
us to reexamine the assumptions
that we hold about America,

02:07:15.160 --> 02:07:18.760
about how wonderful we think
we are, about how great

02:07:18.760 --> 02:07:22.280
we think we are, about
how wonderful a neighbor

02:07:22.280 --> 02:07:26.650
we think we are, about how
good a world citizen we think

02:07:26.650 --> 02:07:31.780
we are, and about how wonderful
we all feel about the good--

02:07:31.780 --> 02:07:34.750
allegedly-- that has
come out of this tragedy.

02:07:34.750 --> 02:07:36.980
I believe that some
good has come out of it.

02:07:36.980 --> 02:07:38.938
But I believe there's a
great deal more that we

02:07:38.938 --> 02:07:42.605
have to challenge America to do
to live up to her true ideals.

02:07:42.605 --> 02:07:43.480
King put it this way.

02:07:43.480 --> 02:07:45.970
Cowardice asks, is it safe.

02:07:45.970 --> 02:07:48.610
Expediency asks, is it politic?

02:07:48.610 --> 02:07:51.070
Vanity asks, is it popular?

02:07:51.070 --> 02:07:53.555
But conscience
asks, is it right?

02:07:53.555 --> 02:07:55.430
And every now and then,
ladies and gentlemen,

02:07:55.430 --> 02:07:58.180
we must take positions that are
neither comfortable, nor safe,

02:07:58.180 --> 02:08:00.040
nor politic, nor convenient.

02:08:00.040 --> 02:08:03.340
But we do it because
our conscience tells us

02:08:03.340 --> 02:08:04.742
that it's right.

02:08:04.742 --> 02:08:06.700
There's an old gospel
song that I love so much.

02:08:06.700 --> 02:08:09.075
Hearing this choir this morning,
my mind went back to it.

02:08:09.075 --> 02:08:11.530
It says, I'm pressing
all the upward way.

02:08:11.530 --> 02:08:14.080
New heights I'm
gaining every day.

02:08:14.080 --> 02:08:16.270
Still praying as
I'm onward bound.

02:08:16.270 --> 02:08:19.530
Lord, plant my feet
on higher ground.

02:08:19.530 --> 02:08:22.240
It says, my heart has
no desire to stay where

02:08:22.240 --> 02:08:24.490
doubts arise and fears dismay.

02:08:24.490 --> 02:08:26.710
Though some may dwell
where these abound,

02:08:26.710 --> 02:08:30.080
my prayer, my aim,
is higher ground.

02:08:30.080 --> 02:08:35.308
What are we doing to attain,
to reach, that higher ground?

02:08:35.308 --> 02:08:36.350
Eric mentioned Morehouse.

02:08:36.350 --> 02:08:41.120
Benjamin Elijah Mays, who taught
Dr. King everything he knew,

02:08:41.120 --> 02:08:42.440
put it like this.

02:08:42.440 --> 02:08:46.490
"The tragedy of life does not
lie in not reaching your goal.

02:08:46.490 --> 02:08:50.710
The tragedy lies in
having no goal to reach."

02:08:50.710 --> 02:08:53.350
It is not a calamity to die
with dreams unfulfilled,

02:08:53.350 --> 02:08:56.140
but it is a calamity
not to dream.

02:08:56.140 --> 02:09:00.340
It is not a disaster to not be
able to capture your ideals,

02:09:00.340 --> 02:09:04.150
but it is a disaster to
have no ideals to capture.

02:09:04.150 --> 02:09:08.690
It is not a disgrace to not be
able to reach all of the stars,

02:09:08.690 --> 02:09:13.720
but it is a disgrace to
have no stars to reach for.

02:09:13.720 --> 02:09:15.610
Not failure.

02:09:15.610 --> 02:09:20.260
Not failure, but low aim is sin.

02:09:20.260 --> 02:09:21.680
Now, why does that matter?

02:09:21.680 --> 02:09:23.110
Because what gets
most of us most

02:09:23.110 --> 02:09:26.650
of the time in our effort
to make America better

02:09:26.650 --> 02:09:31.550
is not that we fail, but
that our aim is too low.

02:09:31.550 --> 02:09:34.000
It's not that the
president fails

02:09:34.000 --> 02:09:37.810
in trying to expand
diversity at MIT,

02:09:37.810 --> 02:09:41.240
it's that, too often,
our aim is too low.

02:09:41.240 --> 02:09:44.090
It's not that we
fail at what we do

02:09:44.090 --> 02:09:47.120
to make our communities better,
it's that our aim is too low.

02:09:47.120 --> 02:09:49.580
How high are we reaching?

02:09:49.580 --> 02:09:53.240
What goals are we setting?

02:09:53.240 --> 02:09:55.620
What are we really
attempting to accomplish?

02:09:55.620 --> 02:09:58.880
It's not the failure that
gets us, it's our low aim.

02:09:58.880 --> 02:10:02.570
And in trying to make MIT and in
trying to make America better,

02:10:02.570 --> 02:10:05.240
when we fail at what
we try, too many of us

02:10:05.240 --> 02:10:08.660
get knocked down and
don't get back up.

02:10:08.660 --> 02:10:10.910
But my grandmother, Big Mama,
said to me all the time,

02:10:10.910 --> 02:10:15.160
Tavis, you can't win
it if you ain't in it.

02:10:15.160 --> 02:10:17.852
Advocacy is not a
spectator sport.

02:10:17.852 --> 02:10:19.310
To make a difference,
honey, you've

02:10:19.310 --> 02:10:22.810
got to get off the sideline
and get involved in the game.

02:10:22.810 --> 02:10:25.360
To make a difference, you got
to reexamine those assumptions.

02:10:25.360 --> 02:10:28.780
To make a difference, you've got
to ask those tough questions.

02:10:28.780 --> 02:10:32.290
Life is like a heart monitor.

02:10:32.290 --> 02:10:35.200
It goes up and down and up
and down and up and down

02:10:35.200 --> 02:10:36.040
and up and down.

02:10:36.040 --> 02:10:37.900
And the ups and
down are all good.

02:10:37.900 --> 02:10:39.622
Don't let that sucker
flatline on you.

02:10:39.622 --> 02:10:41.080
That's what you're
trying to avoid.

02:10:41.080 --> 02:10:45.100
But the ups and downs of trying
to make a contribution, the ups

02:10:45.100 --> 02:10:48.780
and downs of trying to make a
difference is to be expected.

02:10:48.780 --> 02:10:51.170
I told some students the
other day that being black

02:10:51.170 --> 02:10:52.520
is like flying on an airplane.

02:10:52.520 --> 02:10:54.860
Being an American, quite
frankly-- forget being black--

02:10:54.860 --> 02:10:56.720
being an American is
like flat on a plane.

02:10:56.720 --> 02:10:58.620
Forgive the reference.

02:10:58.620 --> 02:10:59.860
What do I mean?

02:10:59.860 --> 02:11:02.730
When you get on that plane,
you've all heard it before.

02:11:02.730 --> 02:11:06.081
They tell you, in
the unlikely event--

02:11:06.081 --> 02:11:10.200
they don't want to
scare you to death.

02:11:10.200 --> 02:11:13.420
They don't want to
mess with your nerves,

02:11:13.420 --> 02:11:16.090
so they tell you, in the
unlikely event of a sudden loss

02:11:16.090 --> 02:11:18.220
of air pressure,
the oxygen mask will

02:11:18.220 --> 02:11:20.513
drop from the compartment
above your head.

02:11:20.513 --> 02:11:22.180
What they tell you
to do with that mask?

02:11:22.180 --> 02:11:24.430
Take the mask and
put it on yourself

02:11:24.430 --> 02:11:28.840
first before you assist the
person seated next to you.

02:11:28.840 --> 02:11:29.980
What's your point, Tavis?

02:11:29.980 --> 02:11:31.810
You can't help nobody
else in America

02:11:31.810 --> 02:11:34.390
unless you first help yourself.

02:11:34.390 --> 02:11:37.150
And that's what these
celebrations are all about,

02:11:37.150 --> 02:11:44.160
getting us, getting us to
reexamine our own assumptions,

02:11:44.160 --> 02:11:48.340
preparing us to ask
the tough questions,

02:11:48.340 --> 02:11:51.370
preparing us, then, to
go out and do something

02:11:51.370 --> 02:11:54.190
to help make black America
and all of America better.

02:11:54.190 --> 02:11:56.980
In the final analysis I
believe-- and it is true--

02:11:56.980 --> 02:12:01.120
that he who starts behind
in the great race of life

02:12:01.120 --> 02:12:06.200
must forever remain
behind or run faster

02:12:06.200 --> 02:12:08.677
than the man in front.

02:12:08.677 --> 02:12:12.170
It's time for all of
us to pick up the pace.

02:12:12.170 --> 02:12:15.164
[APPLAUSE]

02:12:49.170 --> 02:12:52.130
GOMEZ: Thank you, Mr. Smiley,
for your heartfelt words.

02:12:55.890 --> 02:12:59.070
And now, it is my pleasure to
introduce Provost Robert A.

02:12:59.070 --> 02:13:00.390
Brown.

02:13:00.390 --> 02:13:05.190
Provost Brown will recognize
the 2001-2002 Martin Luther King

02:13:05.190 --> 02:13:07.568
Jr. Visiting Professors.

02:13:13.360 --> 02:13:15.400
BROWN: It's really a
pleasure to introduce

02:13:15.400 --> 02:13:18.970
an outstanding group of
colleagues to you this morning.

02:13:18.970 --> 02:13:21.250
The MLK Visiting
Professor Program

02:13:21.250 --> 02:13:24.980
was established at MIT to
bring into our community

02:13:24.980 --> 02:13:28.150
a wonderful group of black
and Hispanic scholars

02:13:28.150 --> 02:13:30.730
to enrich our environment.

02:13:30.730 --> 02:13:34.090
I think today has been a
wonderful opportunity for them

02:13:34.090 --> 02:13:39.940
to share with us really truly
inspirational talks by a couple

02:13:39.940 --> 02:13:43.120
of our students and by Tavis.

02:13:43.120 --> 02:13:47.920
And I think that if we did
give honorary admissions

02:13:47.920 --> 02:13:51.400
to our undergraduate
class, Tavis

02:13:51.400 --> 02:13:54.610
would receive one having
already passed the rhetoric

02:13:54.610 --> 02:13:55.810
requirement.

02:13:55.810 --> 02:13:59.170
[LAUGHTER]

02:13:59.170 --> 02:14:03.160
The MLK program sponsors
faculty and scholars

02:14:03.160 --> 02:14:04.540
all over the Institute.

02:14:04.540 --> 02:14:08.590
We currently have visitors in
the Urban Studies and Planning

02:14:08.590 --> 02:14:10.390
Department, Physics,
Material Science

02:14:10.390 --> 02:14:12.970
and Engineering, Chemistry,
and the Sloan School.

02:14:12.970 --> 02:14:15.130
Several of them were
with us for breakfast.

02:14:15.130 --> 02:14:17.710
A couple have had to
leave because, as is

02:14:17.710 --> 02:14:20.960
a calling in the profession,
if you have a class,

02:14:20.960 --> 02:14:23.240
you must teach.

02:14:23.240 --> 02:14:25.510
And actually, today's
the first day of class.

02:14:25.510 --> 02:14:28.300
And if there is a bad form--

02:14:28.300 --> 02:14:29.850
for some of us.

02:14:29.850 --> 02:14:32.170
And if there is a bad
form for a faculty member,

02:14:32.170 --> 02:14:34.750
it's to be late to the
first day of class.

02:14:34.750 --> 02:14:38.740
Let me begin by introducing
Dr. Edna Ambundo.

02:14:38.740 --> 02:14:42.190
Edna is an MLK Visiting Scholar
in the Department of Chemistry.

02:14:42.190 --> 02:14:44.920
She received her BS degree
from the University of Nairobi

02:14:44.920 --> 02:14:48.760
in Kenya and her PhD from
Wayne State University.

02:14:48.760 --> 02:14:51.610
She is participating in research
and teaching in the Chemistry

02:14:51.610 --> 02:14:53.710
Department in collaboration
with Professor Steve

02:14:53.710 --> 02:14:55.172
Lippert, the department head.

02:14:55.172 --> 02:14:56.645
Welcome, Edna.

02:14:56.645 --> 02:15:00.082
[APPLAUSE]

02:15:02.550 --> 02:15:04.950
Now the next two
colleagues are not with us,

02:15:04.950 --> 02:15:06.870
but I want to
mention their names.

02:15:06.870 --> 02:15:11.400
Otis Jennings is visiting in
the Sloan School of Management.

02:15:11.400 --> 02:15:13.950
He's completed his PhD
in industrial and systems

02:15:13.950 --> 02:15:16.350
engineering at Georgia
Tech University where

02:15:16.350 --> 02:15:19.740
he was an NSF Graduate Fellow
and a Georgia Tech Presidential

02:15:19.740 --> 02:15:20.550
Fellow.

02:15:20.550 --> 02:15:24.210
He received his BS degree in
engineering from Princeton,

02:15:24.210 --> 02:15:27.130
and he's visiting the
Operations Research Center.

02:15:27.130 --> 02:15:31.050
Raul Lejano in the Department
of Urban Studies and Planning

02:15:31.050 --> 02:15:33.690
left a few minutes
ago to go teach.

02:15:33.690 --> 02:15:36.750
He's visiting in urban
studies and planning.

02:15:36.750 --> 02:15:39.450
His PhD is environmental
health science,

02:15:39.450 --> 02:15:41.030
and he's currently
on the faculty

02:15:41.030 --> 02:15:46.590
of UC Irvine in the Department
of Urban and Regional Planning.

02:15:46.590 --> 02:15:49.020
Phillip Thompson with
us from the Department

02:15:49.020 --> 02:15:50.790
of Urban Studies and Planning.

02:15:50.790 --> 02:15:53.700
Phil is an associate
professor of political science

02:15:53.700 --> 02:15:54.720
at Columbia.

02:15:54.720 --> 02:15:55.320
Welcome, Phil.

02:15:55.320 --> 02:15:57.180
[APPLAUSE]

02:15:57.180 --> 02:15:59.160
He earned his PhD
in political science

02:15:59.160 --> 02:16:01.320
from the City
University of New York

02:16:01.320 --> 02:16:04.230
and a BA in psychology
from Harvard University

02:16:04.230 --> 02:16:07.050
and a master's in urban
planning from Hunter College.

02:16:07.050 --> 02:16:09.900
Before joining the
faculty of Columbia,

02:16:09.900 --> 02:16:13.810
he was deputy general manager of
operations of the New York City

02:16:13.810 --> 02:16:16.440
Housing Authority and head of
the mayor's office for housing

02:16:16.440 --> 02:16:19.070
coordination in New York
under Mayor David Dinkins.

02:16:21.600 --> 02:16:26.010
Sekazi Mtingwa is visiting us.

02:16:26.010 --> 02:16:29.730
He's at the Laboratory of
Nuclear Science and Physics

02:16:29.730 --> 02:16:31.320
in the Department of Physics.

02:16:31.320 --> 02:16:33.660
Professor Mtingwa
is an alumni of MIT.

02:16:33.660 --> 02:16:37.590
He holds both a BS in
physics and mathematics

02:16:37.590 --> 02:16:39.840
from the Institute.

02:16:39.840 --> 02:16:44.160
As well, he has a PhD in
physics from Princeton.

02:16:44.160 --> 02:16:47.730
He joined North Carolina
A&amp;T State University

02:16:47.730 --> 02:16:50.700
in the Department
of Physics in 1991

02:16:50.700 --> 02:16:52.860
and served as chair
of the department

02:16:52.860 --> 02:16:54.660
from '91 through '94.

02:16:54.660 --> 02:16:56.969
He's also taught at the
University of Illinois

02:16:56.969 --> 02:16:59.190
in Chicago, the
University of Rochester,

02:16:59.190 --> 02:17:01.980
and Morgan State University.

02:17:01.980 --> 02:17:06.410
Finally, let me introduce
Professor Eni Njoko--

02:17:06.410 --> 02:17:10.958
[APPLAUSE]

02:17:10.958 --> 02:17:12.750
--who's visiting in
the Department of Civil

02:17:12.750 --> 02:17:15.780
and Environmental Engineering.

02:17:15.780 --> 02:17:17.580
Eni is also an alumnus of MIT.

02:17:17.580 --> 02:17:20.459
He received his PhD
and his SM degree here,

02:17:20.459 --> 02:17:22.379
both in civil engineering.

02:17:22.379 --> 02:17:24.540
He holds a BA degree
in natural sciences

02:17:24.540 --> 02:17:27.030
and electrical sciences
from Cambridge University

02:17:27.030 --> 02:17:29.610
in England.

02:17:29.610 --> 02:17:32.280
He is currently a research
scientist at the Jet Propulsion

02:17:32.280 --> 02:17:34.530
Laboratory where he's a
principal investigator

02:17:34.530 --> 02:17:37.709
specializing in satellite
imaging for oil moisture

02:17:37.709 --> 02:17:41.790
estimation and the technology
of large microwave radiometric

02:17:41.790 --> 02:17:42.959
antennas.

02:17:42.959 --> 02:17:48.170
He's also taught at Harvey Mudd
University while being at JPL.

02:17:48.170 --> 02:17:50.130
Welcome to you all.

02:17:50.130 --> 02:17:52.580
[APPLAUSE]

02:18:03.395 --> 02:18:05.520
GOMEZ: We will now continue
with some announcements

02:18:05.520 --> 02:18:07.493
relevant to the celebration.

02:18:07.493 --> 02:18:09.660
I would like to announce
that the Martin Luther King

02:18:09.660 --> 02:18:13.049
Committee presents the student
dedication to the Martin Luther

02:18:13.049 --> 02:18:16.500
King installation to commemorate
Dr. Martin Luther King.

02:18:16.500 --> 02:18:21.000
Again, that's in Lobby 10, so
I invite you all to go see it.

02:18:21.000 --> 02:18:23.950
Now I would like to ask Reverend
Amy McCreath to come forward

02:18:23.950 --> 02:18:28.236
to give us the benediction to
close this morning's program.

02:18:36.188 --> 02:18:38.673
MCCREATH: Would
you please stand?

02:18:48.629 --> 02:18:52.049
Let us go forth with
courage equipped

02:18:52.049 --> 02:18:55.559
for the work of doing
justice, empowered

02:18:55.559 --> 02:19:02.530
with skills and vision to heal,
to reconcile, and to liberate.

02:19:02.530 --> 02:19:08.590
Let us go forth in peace to be
the peace we seek, confident

02:19:08.590 --> 02:19:12.790
that what unites us is more
powerful and eternal than what

02:19:12.790 --> 02:19:16.639
separates us one from another.

02:19:16.639 --> 02:19:22.430
Let us go forth together
locking arms and joining hearts,

02:19:22.430 --> 02:19:24.350
strengthening one
another for the work

02:19:24.350 --> 02:19:28.969
ahead, celebrating what
we accomplished together.

02:19:28.969 --> 02:19:32.840
Together.

02:19:32.840 --> 02:19:36.790
Let us go forth in joy,
reveling in the good news

02:19:36.790 --> 02:19:42.610
that our creator has called us
at this holy time to holy work

02:19:42.610 --> 02:19:46.480
and confident that
love will prevail.

02:19:46.480 --> 02:19:51.490
May justice roll down our
limbs and through our veins

02:19:51.490 --> 02:19:56.590
and pour out from our mouths
and be the fruit of our labors

02:19:56.590 --> 02:20:01.928
that righteousness and peace
may abide like a mighty stream.

02:20:01.928 --> 02:20:02.785
Amen.

02:20:02.785 --> 02:20:03.410
AUDIENCE: Amen.

02:20:10.340 --> 02:20:12.733
GOMEZ: I hope you have enjoyed
this morning's program.

02:20:12.733 --> 02:20:14.150
It's been a wonderful
event, which

02:20:14.150 --> 02:20:16.768
I'm sure will remain in our
thoughts for a long time.

02:20:16.768 --> 02:20:17.810
Thank you all for coming.

02:20:17.810 --> 02:20:19.670
And I hope to see you next year.

02:20:19.670 --> 02:20:22.460
This concludes our 20th
Annual Martin Luther King Jr.

02:20:22.460 --> 02:20:24.260
Celebration.

02:20:24.260 --> 02:20:26.110
Thank you.

